insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (3145, $q$When the War Enters Your Tent$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=3145$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/when-the-war-enters-your-tent$q$, $q$2027-12-05T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$It is easier to talk about courage when the danger belongs to someone else. Then a crisis crosses the threshold, and neutrality stops feeling like a safe place to stand.

Jael (JAY-el) was the wife of Heber the Kenite. Her family lived in tents near Kedesh in the hill country of northern Canaan. Heber’s household had peaceful relations with Jabin, the Canaanite king whose commander, Sisera, had oppressed Israel for twenty years (Judges 4:2–3, 11, 17).

After Deborah sent Barak into battle, Sisera’s chariots were routed near Mount Tabor and the Kishon River. He abandoned his chariot and fled on foot to Jael’s tent. The place probably looked safe because of the alliance with her household (Judges 4:14–17).

Jael welcomed him, covered him, and gave him milk when he asked for water. Sisera then told her to deny that anyone was inside. While he slept from exhaustion, Jael took a tent peg and hammer and killed him. When Barak arrived, she showed him the body (Judges 4:18–22). Deborah’s victory song later praised Jael and retold the killing without softening it (Judges 5:24–27).

This is not a gentle story, and it should not be turned into a cheerful slogan about using whatever tool is nearby. The text places Jael inside war, oppression, divided loyalties, and lethal choices. It records the deliverance of Israel while leaving modern readers to feel the violence rather than pretending it is tidy.

Most of us will never face anything comparable. Still, we recognize the moment when a problem we hoped to avoid enters our space. A colleague asks us to hide misconduct. A friend expects silence about harm. A powerful person assumes our hospitality includes cooperation. The decision is no longer theoretical. Silence has consequences in that moment.

Jael’s story makes me ask what my apparent neutrality protects. Avoiding involvement can feel peaceful while quietly serving the person who already holds power. Yet action also carries responsibility, and urgency does not remove the need to see the human cost clearly.

We should be cautious about claiming Jael as a simple model for our choices. Her world is not ours. But her tent reminds us that character becomes visible when danger arrives close enough to demand an answer. At that threshold, courage needs more than intensity. It needs a truthful understanding of whom our silence would shelter.$pt$, 3, null, $q$waiting$q$)
