insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (3065, $q$Learn It Before You Teach It$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=3065$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/learn-it-before-you-teach-it$q$, $q$2027-09-12T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Advice becomes dangerous when the speaker has not done the work behind it. A polished explanation can hide shallow study. A public standard can hide private exceptions. The gap may stay invisible until other people begin building their lives on our words.

Ezra (EZ-ruh) was a Jewish priest and scribe living under Persian rule. His family belonged to the people whose parents and grandparents had been taken from Judah to Babylon. In the seventh year of King Artaxerxes, Ezra received permission to lead a group from Babylon to Jerusalem, a journey the book places across about four months (Ezra 7:1–9). View the map for this story.

Jerusalem was being rebuilt after exile, but buildings were only part of the repair. The returning community also needed to understand the law that shaped its worship and shared life.

Ezra 7:10 gives the order of his work: he “had devoted himself to the study and observance of the Law of the Lord, and to teaching its decrees and laws in Israel” (NIV).

Study came first. Ezra did not treat inherited words as decoration. He gave attention to what they said.

Practice came next. Knowledge was not enough. The teacher stood under the same instruction he would explain to other people.

Then came teaching. Ezra’s authority was connected to learning and obedience, not merely to his title or confidence.

That sequence does not make a leader perfect. Ezra’s later response to intermarriage in the community is difficult and should not be turned into a quick modern formula. The account involves covenant identity, vulnerable families, and decisions whose human cost is recorded but not fully explored (Ezra 9–10). Careful readers should resist using it to support prejudice or to make complicated families into symbols.

Even so, Ezra 7:10 offers a clear test for anyone whose work depends on words. Have I studied enough to know what I am claiming? Am I willing to live under the standard I recommend? Can I teach without pretending that my interpretation settles every question?

The pattern applies beyond a pulpit or classroom. A manager explains a policy. A parent sets a boundary. A friend offers counsel. In each case, credibility grows when words have passed through honest attention and personal practice.

I want to slow the order down. Learn before speaking. Practice before prescribing. Teach with enough humility to keep learning.

That kind of leadership may sound less impressive. It is also far safer to trust.$pt$, 9, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (3067, $q$Ambition Can Learn to Serve$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=3067$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/ambition-can-learn-to-serve$q$, $q$2027-09-05T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Ambition is not always loud. It can hide inside a desire to matter, to be trusted, or to stand close to someone important. The desire itself may contain courage and energy. The question is what definition of greatness will direct it.

James, son of Zebedee (JAYMZ), was a Galilean fisherman and the brother of John. Jesus called the brothers while they were mending nets in their father’s boat near the Sea of Galilee, in today’s northern Israel. They left the boat and followed him (Mark 1:19–20). View the map for this story.

Jesus nicknamed James and John “Sons of Thunder” (Mark 3:17). Luke records one reason the name fit. When a Samaritan village refused to welcome Jesus, the brothers asked whether they should call down fire to destroy it. Jesus rebuked them, and the group went elsewhere (Luke 9:51–56).

Their intensity also reached for rank. James and John asked Jesus to let them sit at his right and left when he came into glory. The other disciples became angry, perhaps because the request exposed a competition they all understood (Mark 10:35–41).

Jesus did not tell James to stop caring about a meaningful life. He changed the measure. Rulers often make their authority felt, he said, but “whoever wants to become great among you must be your servant” (Mark 10:42–43, NIV). Greatness would be shaped by responsibility and sacrifice, not proximity to the highest seat.

James’s later work is mostly hidden from the New Testament. We are not given a series of scenes showing exactly how he grew. Acts records only that King Herod Agrippa I had James killed with the sword, around AD 44. He became the first of the Twelve whose death is reported in Scripture (Acts 12:1–2).

We should not fill that silence with invented speeches or pretend martyrdom proves every earlier flaw vanished. We can say that the ambitious fisherman stayed with the mission when following Jesus became costly.

That gives me a better question for my own ambition. Am I trying to be near importance, or am I becoming useful to people? Would I still do the work if someone else received the title? Can my intensity protect rather than punish?

Ambition needs more than restraint. It needs direction. James’s story leaves the change partly unseen, but Jesus’s standard is clear.

The best seat is not the measure of a life. The willingness to serve from whatever seat we are given is.$pt$, 10, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (3071, $q$Pain Does Not Excuse Control$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=3071$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/pain-does-not-excuse-control$q$, $q$2027-08-22T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$A real grievance can become dangerous when it starts granting permission for anything. Being wronged does not make every response right. If pain becomes a license to manipulate, punish, or control, more people begin carrying the damage.

Absalom (AB-suh-luhm) was a son of King David, born in Hebron before David made Jerusalem his capital. He was also the full brother of Tamar. When their half brother Amnon raped Tamar, David became furious, but the account records no action against Amnon (2 Samuel 13:1–22). View the map for this story.

Absalom’s anger had a reason. His sister had been violated, and the king who was also their father failed to deliver visible justice.

Two years later, Absalom arranged a feast, ordered his servants to kill Amnon, and fled to his maternal grandfather in Geshur. After three years he returned to Jerusalem, but David would not see him. Two more years passed before father and son met (2 Samuel 13:23–39; 14:21–33).

The long delay did not produce repair. Absalom began building public support. He stood beside the road to the city gate, listened to people bringing cases to the king, told them their claims were valid, and suggested that he would give them justice if he were appointed judge. He greeted people with a kiss. In this way, the text says, he “stole the hearts of the people of Israel” (2 Samuel 15:1–6, NIV).

Then Absalom went to Hebron, about nineteen miles south of Jerusalem, and declared himself king. David fled the capital. The grievance inside one family had grown into a national conflict (2 Samuel 15:7–14).

Absalom’s story should not be used to excuse David. David’s failures toward Tamar, Amnon, and Absalom remain part of the account. But neglected justice did not make murder, manipulation, or a coup into justice.

That distinction is uncomfortable because pain often strengthens moral certainty. I may be accurate about what someone did and still be wrong about what I am entitled to do next.

A healthier response can include naming the harm, creating distance, seeking accountable help, protecting others, and pursuing lawful consequences. It does not require pretending reconciliation has happened. It does require refusing to make control the proof that justice has returned.

Absalom eventually died in battle, and David mourned him as a son (2 Samuel 18:9–17, 33). No one in the story escaped the cost.

Pain deserves truth and care. It does not deserve the authority to choose every next step.$pt$, 11, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2974, $q$Seen After Being Sent Away$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2974$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/seen-after-being-sent-away$q$, $q$2027-08-15T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Being ignored can make a person feel smaller than the problem around them. Someone else makes the decision, holds the money, or controls the door. The person with the least power is left carrying the largest cost.

Hagar (HAY-gahr) was an Egyptian slave in the household of Abram and Sarai, later called Abraham and Sarah. Sarai had no children, so she gave Hagar to Abram in hopes of building a family through her. Hagar became pregnant, conflict followed, and Sarai mistreated her. Hagar ran away (Genesis 16:1–6). View the map for this story.

An angel of the Lord found her beside a spring on the road to Shur. Hagar explained that she was fleeing. The angel told her to return, but also promised that her descendants would be too many to count. Her son would be named Ishmael, meaning “God hears,” because God had heard her misery (Genesis 16:7–12).

Then Hagar gave God a name: “You are the God who sees me” (Genesis 16:13, NIV). The nearby well became known as Beer Lahai Roi, the well of the Living One who sees me. Its exact location is uncertain, somewhere in the wilderness south of Canaan between Kadesh and Bered.

Years later, Sarah demanded that Hagar and Ishmael be sent away. Abraham gave them food and water, and Hagar wandered in the Desert of Beersheba. When the water was gone, she placed Ishmael under a bush and moved away because she could not bear to watch him die (Genesis 21:8–16).

God heard the boy crying. The angel called Hagar by name, repeated the promise about Ishmael, and God opened her eyes to a well. She filled the water skin and gave her son a drink. Ishmael grew up in the desert, and Hagar found him a wife from Egypt (Genesis 21:17–21).

Being seen did not make the harm unreal. Hagar was still enslaved, mistreated, and displaced by decisions made above her. But those decisions did not define her worth or erase her future.

That matters whenever leadership turns people into tools. A worker absorbs an impossible schedule. A caregiver is left out of the family decision. Someone with less status is expected to stay quiet because speaking would be costly.

I cannot fix every wilderness, but I can ask who is carrying the cost of my choice. I can listen before deciding, share power where I can, and refuse to treat another person as expendable.

Hagar’s story leaves us with a better question than whether overlooked people matter. The question is whether we will learn to see them before a crisis forces us to look.$pt$, 12, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (3069, $q$Grief Can Name Blessing Again$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=3069$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/grief-can-name-blessing-again$q$, $q$2027-08-01T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Some encouragement arrives too quickly. It asks a grieving person to find the lesson, count the blessings, or move forward before the loss has even found words. Naomi’s story makes room for a slower kind of hope. View the map for this story.

Naomi (nay-OH-mee) lived in Bethlehem during the time of Israel’s judges. When famine struck, she went with her husband, Elimelek, and their two sons to Moab, east of the Dead Sea in today’s Jordan. Her husband died there. Her sons married Ruth and Orpah, two Moabite women, and then both sons died as well (Ruth 1:1–5).

Naomi heard that food had returned to Judah, so she started home. She urged her daughters-in-law to remain in Moab and build new lives. Orpah stayed. Ruth refused to leave Naomi and traveled with her to Bethlehem (Ruth 1:6–18).

When the town’s women recognized Naomi, she did not give them a polished answer. “Don’t call me Naomi,” she said. “Call me Mara, because the Almighty has made my life very bitter” (Ruth 1:20, NIV). Naomi means pleasant; Mara means bitter.

The book does not rebuke her for saying it. It lets grief speak in public.

Naomi could not yet see how Ruth’s loyalty would reshape their future. Ruth gathered leftover grain in the field of Boaz, a relative of Elimelek. Naomi recognized that connection and helped Ruth understand the family custom that could provide security and preserve the family line (Ruth 2:17–23; 3:1–5).

Boaz married Ruth. Their son Obed became the grandfather of King David. At the child’s birth, the women of Bethlehem spoke blessing over Naomi. They said Ruth, who loved her, was worth more to her than seven sons. Naomi held the baby and cared for him (Ruth 4:13–17).

The ending does not return her husband or sons. Obed is not a replacement for the people who died. Hope is not an argument that the losses were secretly good.

What changes is Naomi’s ability to receive life alongside grief. She speaks bitterness, accepts Ruth’s companionship, makes a practical plan, and eventually holds a child whose future she could not have predicted.

That pace matters. I do not need to force hope into someone else’s first sentence. I can listen, bring food, handle one task, or stay close without explaining the pain.

Grief may keep its name. In time, blessing can have a name too. Naomi’s story allows both words to remain true.$pt$, 13, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2962, $q$Loyalty Cannot Excuse Everything$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2962$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/loyalty-cannot-excuse-everything$q$, $q$2027-06-20T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Loyalty can become a flattering name for helping someone avoid consequences. We tell ourselves we are protecting the leader, the family, or the team. Over time, solving every threat with secrecy or harm can make our “help” more dangerous than the original problem.

Joab (JOH-ab) was King David’s nephew and commander of Israel’s army. He was capable, courageous, and central to David’s rise. He was also willing to use violence when others chose restraint. View the map for this story.

Early in David’s reign, Joab’s brother Asahel died while pursuing Abner, commander of a rival force. Abner had warned Asahel to turn aside. Later, after Abner made peace with David, Joab drew him aside and killed him (2 Samuel 2:18–23; 3:20–27).

David publicly mourned Abner and said Joab’s violence had happened without his approval. Yet Joab remained commander (2 Samuel 3:28–39).

That unresolved pattern continued. When David arranged Uriah’s death, Joab carried out the order by placing him in deadly fighting and withdrawing support (2 Samuel 11:14–17). When David later ordered the army to be gentle with his rebellious son Absalom, Joab killed Absalom after finding him helpless and caught in a tree (2 Samuel 18:5, 9–15).

Joab could also tell David truths others avoided. He confronted the king after Absalom’s death when David’s public grief humiliated the soldiers who had risked their lives for him (2 Samuel 19:1–8).

That complexity matters. Joab was not merely disloyal or loyal, wise or violent. His service contained all of it. The Bible lets us see skill and usefulness without asking us to call every method good.

Leaders help create this danger when they keep rewarding results while postponing accountability for how those results were achieved. David benefited from Joab’s decisiveness, distanced himself from Joab’s bloodshed, and still left the reckoning for his son Solomon (1 Kings 2:5–6).

I need to ask what my loyalty is protecting. Is it protecting a person from unfair attack, or protecting that person from the truth? Does my help preserve a mission, or excuse methods that betray it?

Real loyalty can say no. It can refuse an order, name a boundary, and tell a friend that usefulness does not cancel harm.

Joab solved many problems for David. The violence inside those solutions became a problem neither man could leave behind. Skill and courage matter, but character must govern both. Otherwise, today’s indispensable fixer can become tomorrow’s deepest liability.$pt$, 14, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2960, $q$What We Hide Still Spreads$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2960$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/what-we-hide-still-spreads$q$, $q$2027-06-13T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$A secret can feel contained because no one else knows it. But hidden choices still change budgets, trust, safety, and the decisions other people make. The secrecy may be private. The cost rarely is.

Achan (AY-kan) was an Israelite from the tribe of Judah during Joshua’s campaign in Canaan. Before Jericho fell, Israel was told that the city’s silver, gold, bronze, and iron belonged in God’s treasury. Other devoted things were not to be taken (Joshua 6:17–19). View the map for this story.

After the victory, Israel attacked the smaller city of Ai. The force was defeated, and about thirty-six men died. Joshua grieved and asked why God had brought the people across the Jordan only for this to happen (Joshua 7:2–9).

God’s answer connected the defeat to what had been hidden after Jericho. Israel had taken devoted things, stolen, lied, and placed the goods among personal possessions (Joshua 7:10–12).

The search narrowed by tribe, clan, family, and household until Achan was identified. Joshua asked him to tell the truth. Achan confessed that he had seen a beautiful robe from Babylonia, silver, and gold. He coveted them, took them, and hid them in the ground beneath his tent (Joshua 7:16–21).

Messengers found the items exactly where he said they were.

The chapter ends in severe judgment on Achan and his household (Joshua 7:22–26). The passage raises difficult questions about collective responsibility and punishment. We should not flatten those questions into a quick slogan or use this story to justify punishing families for one person’s wrongdoing.

The narrower warning is still clear: Achan’s hidden act changed what happened to other people. Soldiers entered battle without knowing the community was carrying a breach they had not addressed.

Modern secrets can work that way. A falsified number shapes someone else’s plan. Hidden harassment changes who feels safe. Money quietly redirected leaves another person explaining why the work cannot be completed.

Accountability should not become suspicion of everyone. It should create honest ways to surface problems early: clear records, shared oversight, safe reporting, and leaders willing to hear bad news.

When I am tempted to call a compromise private, I need to ask who could eventually carry its cost. Confession cannot undo every consequence, but bringing truth into the open is where repair can begin.

What we bury still occupies ground. What we hide still spreads through families, teams, congregations, and communities that trusted us.$pt$, 15, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2958, $q$When People Become Pieces$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2958$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/when-people-become-pieces$q$, $q$2027-06-06T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Power becomes dangerous when it stops seeing people as people. A relationship turns into leverage. A marriage becomes a message. Someone’s pain is treated as acceptable because it helps another person win.

Michal (MIH-kal) was a daughter of King Saul and the first wife of David. First Samuel says she loved David. Saul saw that love as an opportunity to place David in danger, setting a risky bride-price in the hope that David would die fighting the Philistines (1 Samuel 18:20–25). View the map for this story.

The plan failed, and Michal married David. Later, when Saul sent men to kill him, she warned David, lowered him through a window, and used a household idol and bedding to delay the pursuers. Her action saved his life (1 Samuel 19:11–17).

Then Michal’s own choices narrowed. Saul gave her to another man, Palti. Years later, after Saul’s death, David demanded her return as part of negotiations over the kingdom. Palti followed her, weeping, until he was ordered to go home (1 Samuel 25:44; 2 Samuel 3:13–16).

The text reports these transfers without telling us what Michal wanted at either point. That silence should make us careful. We can describe what powerful men did without inventing her private response.

Later, Michal watched from a window as David danced before the ark entering Jerusalem. She despised him in her heart and confronted him when he returned. David defended his celebration sharply. Their exchange ends with the statement that Michal had no children (2 Samuel 6:16–23).

Readers debate how to judge that final conflict. Michal’s contempt is plainly present. So is a life repeatedly shaped by her father’s fear, David’s rise, and royal claims made around her.

Her story makes me ask how often a person’s difficult response is treated as the whole story while the history of pressure behind it disappears.

That does not excuse contempt or harmful speech. It does challenge me to hold accountability and context together. In a family, workplace, or church, people should never become symbols of loyalty or tools for settling someone else’s contest.

I can resist that by asking who has a voice, who bears the cost, and whether a decision protects the dignity of the people most affected.

Michal was not merely Saul’s daughter, David’s wife, or a figure at a window. She was a person inside those relationships. Good leadership remembers that before making its next move.$pt$, 16, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2956, $q$Repentance Has a Receipt$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2956$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/repentance-has-a-receipt$q$, $q$2027-05-30T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$An apology can describe regret without changing what the other person lost. Repair asks a harder question: what can I return, replace, correct, or make right? Zacchaeus did not leave that question vague. View the map for this story.

Zacchaeus (za-KEE-uhs) lived in Jericho, near the Jordan Valley in today’s West Bank. Luke calls him a chief tax collector and a wealthy man. Tax collectors worked inside Rome’s system and were widely distrusted because the work could reward overcollection (Luke 19:1–2).

When Jesus passed through Jericho, Zacchaeus wanted to see him. He was short and could not see over the crowd, so he ran ahead and climbed a sycamore-fig tree (Luke 19:3–4).

Jesus stopped, looked up, called him by name, and said he must stay at Zacchaeus’s house. Zacchaeus came down and welcomed him gladly. The crowd complained that Jesus had gone to be the guest of a sinner (Luke 19:5–7).

Then Zacchaeus stood and made his response measurable. He said he would give half his possessions to the poor. If he had cheated anyone, he would repay four times the amount (Luke 19:8).

The wording “if” does not require us to decide how many people he had cheated. What matters is that he named a process for repair. The people affected would not have to settle for a speech about his intentions.

Jesus answered, “Today salvation has come to this house,” and described his mission as seeking and saving the lost (Luke 19:9–10, NIV).

I notice the order. Jesus offered Zacchaeus welcome before Zacchaeus announced restitution. Grace did not pretend harm was unimportant. It gave him a place from which to face it.

That pattern can guide an ordinary apology. First, name what I did without shrinking it. Then listen for the cost to the other person. Correct the record, return the money, replace the damaged item, or change the process that allowed the harm. Some losses cannot be repaid, and forgiveness cannot be purchased. Still, concrete repair can show that regret has moved beyond words.

Zacchaeus also made his change public enough to be accountable. He attached proportions and consequences to it.

I may not need a dramatic promise, but I do need a next step someone else could recognize. Repentance becomes credible when it reaches beyond my feelings to the people who carried the cost.

A changed heart is not a receipt. Repair is where the numbers begin to show.$pt$, 17, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2824, $q$Do the Necessary Next Thing$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2824$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/do-the-necessary-next-thing$q$, $q$2027-05-23T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Pressure can leave us waiting for certainty that will not arrive in time. A child is in danger. A serious mistake comes to light. Someone must call for help, admit what happened, or take the next safe step. Courage is not always a grand plan. Sometimes it is doing the necessary thing in front of us.

Zipporah (ZIP-er-uh) was the wife of Moses. She first appears after Moses fled Egypt and settled with her family in Midian. Ancient Midian is usually placed east of the Gulf of Aqaba, in parts of today’s northwestern Saudi Arabia and southern Jordan, though its exact boundaries remain uncertain. The map page shows that wider region rather than claiming an exact home.

Zipporah’s father, Reuel, welcomed Moses after he helped the family’s daughters at a well. Reuel later gave Zipporah to Moses in marriage, and she gave birth to a son named Gershom (Exodus 2:15–22).

Years later, Moses started back toward Egypt with his wife and sons. God had called him to confront Pharaoh and lead the Israelites out of slavery. During the journey, the family stopped for the night. Then the story becomes startling: “the Lord met Moses and was about to kill him” (Exodus 4:24, NIV).

The passage is only three verses long, and it leaves important questions unanswered. It does not explain why the required circumcision had been delayed or what every gesture and phrase meant. We should not pretend the text gives us certainty it does not give.

What it does show is Zipporah acting. She took a flint knife, circumcised her son, touched Moses’ feet, and spoke of a “bridegroom of blood.” Then the danger passed (Exodus 4:25–26). Whatever else the difficult scene means, her decisive action stands at its center.

Later, Exodus says Moses had sent Zipporah and their two sons away. It does not say when or why. Her father brought them back to Moses after Israel left Egypt, and the family was reunited (Exodus 18:1–6).

Acting under pressure is not the same as acting without care. Panic grabs at anything. Courage looks for the next responsible step. It calls the doctor, tells the truth, moves someone away from danger, or asks a wiser person for help.

We may still have questions afterward. Zipporah’s story certainly leaves us with some. But we do not need every question answered before we protect what has been placed in our care. When the moment is urgent, faithfulness may begin with the next necessary thing.$pt$, 18, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2954, $q$Integrity When Power Fails$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2954$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/integrity-when-power-fails$q$, $q$2027-05-16T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Integrity does not guarantee protection. Sometimes the person who keeps faith is harmed by someone with more power. That is not a reason to abandon integrity. It is a reason to build leadership that never treats another person’s character as something to exploit.

Uriah the Hittite (you-RAI-uh) was a soldier in King David’s army and the husband of Bathsheba. His label “the Hittite” marks him as having foreign ancestry or family identity, yet he served among David’s elite warriors (2 Samuel 23:39). View the map for this story.

While Uriah was away with the army besieging Rabbah—modern Amman, Jordan—David saw Bathsheba in Jerusalem and slept with her. When she became pregnant, David summoned Uriah home from the battle (2 Samuel 11:1–6).

David told Uriah to go home. The plan would make the child appear to be Uriah’s. But Uriah slept at the palace entrance with the king’s servants instead.

When David asked why, Uriah pointed to the ark, Israel’s troops, and his commander Joab camping in the open. He would not enjoy home while they remained in the field (2 Samuel 11:7–11).

David tried again, even getting Uriah drunk. Uriah still did not go home (2 Samuel 11:12–13).

Then David sent him back to the army carrying a sealed message for Joab. The message ordered Joab to place Uriah where the fighting was fiercest and withdraw support so he would be killed. Joab obeyed, and Uriah died (2 Samuel 11:14–17).

The story gives Uriah no awareness of the plot. We should not invent noble speeches or private thoughts for him. His observable choices are enough. He acted in solidarity with people still facing danger, while his king used that loyalty as part of a cover-up.

This is why integrity and accountability belong together. Organizations cannot simply praise loyal people. They must create ways to protect them from leaders who may misuse trust, secrecy, or obedience.

If I lead, I need people who can question me and systems I cannot quietly bend. If I serve, integrity may also require raising a concern, preserving evidence, or asking for help outside the chain of command.

Nathan later told David the truth, and David admitted, “I have sinned against the Lord” (2 Samuel 12:13, NIV). Accountability finally reached the king—but not before Uriah paid the cost.

Integrity is precious. Good power protects it instead of turning it into a weapon.$pt$, 19, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2835, $q$Orders Do Not Remove Responsibility$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2835$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/orders-do-not-remove-responsibility$q$, $q$2027-05-09T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$An order can make a wrong choice feel smaller. Someone with more power decided what should happen, so we tell ourselves the responsibility belongs to that person. But authority does not carry the whole moral weight of what our hands do.

Doeg the Edomite (DOH-g) served Saul, Israel’s first king, as his chief shepherd. He appears at Nob, a town of priests probably near Jerusalem, though its exact site remains uncertain. The map page shows the proposed area north of the city without claiming a confirmed location.

David had fled from Saul. At Nob, he asked the priest Ahimelek for food and a weapon. David claimed he was on the king’s business, so Ahimelek gave him consecrated bread and Goliath’s sword. Doeg was there and saw the exchange (1 Samuel 21:1–9).

Later, Saul accused his officials of hiding David’s actions. Doeg spoke up. He reported that Ahimelek had helped David. Saul summoned the priest and his family. Ahimelek explained that he knew nothing about David’s flight and had treated him as a trusted member of the king’s household (1 Samuel 22:6–15).

Saul ordered his guards to kill the priests. They refused. Then Saul gave the order to Doeg.

Doeg obeyed. He killed eighty-five priests and then attacked Nob, including its families and animals. One priest, Abiathar, escaped and reached David (1 Samuel 22:16–21). The story does not soften Saul’s command, and it does not treat Doeg as a tool without a choice. Other servants heard the same king and would not take part.

That difference matters. We may never face an order so terrible, but smaller versions appear at work and at home. A supervisor asks us to hide a safety concern. A leader wants an unfair report changed. A friend pressures us to pass along something meant to hurt another person. “I was told to” explains the pressure. It does not erase our part.

Responsibility may begin with a pause. What harm will this cause? What truth is being left out? Can I refuse, ask for the order in writing, or seek help from someone with the courage to challenge it?

Power can make obedience costly to resist. Doeg’s story does not deny that pressure. It shows why conscience matters most when an authority would like to borrow ours. Someone else may give the order, but I still have to answer for the choice I make.$pt$, 20, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2952, $q$Faithful Without the Spotlight$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2952$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/faithful-without-the-spotlight$q$, $q$2027-05-02T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$We often measure a life by how much material it leaves behind. We count years, accomplishments, followers, and recorded words. Abel’s story offers almost none of that, yet the Bible refuses to call his life insignificant. View the map for this story.

Abel (AY-buhl) was the second son of Adam and Eve and the younger brother of Cain. Genesis describes him simply as a keeper of flocks. Cain worked the soil (Genesis 4:1–2).

In time, both brothers brought offerings to God. Cain brought some of the fruits of the soil. Abel brought fat portions from some of the firstborn of his flock. God looked with favor on Abel and his offering, but not on Cain and his offering (Genesis 4:3–5).

Genesis does not give a complete explanation of that difference. Later Scripture says Abel offered by faith and that his offering was better than Cain’s (Hebrews 11:4). The story does not authorize us to invent details it does not supply.

Cain became angry. God warned him that sin was crouching at his door and that he must rule over it. Cain later took Abel into a field and killed him (Genesis 4:5–8).

Abel speaks no recorded words. He does not defend himself, build a movement, or get the chance to explain his offering. God still hears what violence tried to silence: “Your brother’s blood cries out to me from the ground” (Genesis 4:10, NIV).

That line matters to anyone whose good work feels unseen. Recognition is not what gives faithfulness its value. A caregiver may repeat the same quiet tasks each day. An employee may tell the truth in a room that rewards convenience. A friend may keep showing up without ever becoming central to the story.

Abel’s death also keeps us from romanticizing hidden service. Being unnoticed by people can leave someone vulnerable. Faithfulness deserves protection, and violence deserves to be named rather than covered with spiritual language.

Jesus later referred to “righteous Abel” when speaking about innocent blood (Matthew 23:35). Hebrews says Abel still speaks, even though he is dead (Hebrews 11:4).

His life was brief, but its meaning was not. I do not need a large platform before honesty, worship, or careful work matters. I can bring the best I have to the work in front of me.

The spotlight may never arrive. Faithfulness is still visible where it counts, and its quiet witness can outlive us.$pt$, 21, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2950, $q$The Help Pride Almost Missed$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2950$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/the-help-pride-almost-missed$q$, $q$2027-04-25T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Help is easy to miss when we have already decided what it should look like. We may expect an expert, a dramatic plan, or an answer that confirms our importance. Then the useful advice arrives through an ordinary person and sounds almost too simple.

Naaman (NAY-uh-muhn) commanded the army of Aram, a kingdom centered around Damascus in modern Syria. Second Kings calls him a great man and a valiant soldier. It also says he had a serious skin disease (2 Kings 5:1). View the map for this story.

The first person to point him toward help was an unnamed Israelite girl captured in an Aramean raid. She served Naaman’s wife and said the prophet in Samaria could heal him (2 Kings 5:2–3).

Naaman went to Israel with horses, chariots, expensive gifts, and a letter from his king. But the prophet Elisha did not come out to greet him. A messenger told Naaman to wash seven times in the Jordan River (2 Kings 5:4–10).

Naaman became angry. He had imagined Elisha standing before him, calling on God, and waving a hand over the diseased skin. He also considered the rivers of Damascus better than the Jordan. He turned away in a rage (2 Kings 5:11–12).

Then servants helped him again. They respectfully asked whether he would have done some difficult thing if the prophet had required it. Why not do the simple thing he had been told? Naaman listened, dipped himself seven times in the Jordan, and was healed (2 Kings 5:13–14).

The powerful commander received his direction from a captive girl, a prophet’s messenger, and his own servants. Every one of them stood below him in the social order. Any one of them could have been ignored.

I can recognize that temptation. I may ask for feedback while quietly ranking the people allowed to give it. I may dismiss a small solution because it does not feel equal to the size of my problem.

Listening does not mean accepting every suggestion. It means judging the message on its truth and usefulness, not merely on the status of the person speaking.

Naaman’s rank could organize an army, but it could not heal him. He needed enough humility to follow a simple instruction and enough wisdom to let servants change his mind.

The help we need may already be in the room. Pride asks whether the voice is impressive. Humility asks whether it is telling the truth.$pt$, 22, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2948, $q$Leadership Still Needs Correction$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2948$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/leadership-still-needs-correction$q$, $q$2027-04-18T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Years of faithful work can quietly turn into a shield. We remember the risks we took, the people we helped, and the times we were right. Then correction feels less like guidance and more like disrespect.

Miriam (MIHR-ih-uhm) had a record worth honoring. As a girl in Egypt, she watched over her baby brother Moses after their mother placed him in a basket among the reeds of the Nile. When Pharaoh’s daughter found him, Miriam offered to find a Hebrew woman to nurse him, helping return Moses to his own mother for a time (Exodus 2:1–10). View the map for this story.

Years later, after Israel escaped Egypt and crossed the sea, Miriam was called a prophet. She took a tambourine, led the women in music and dancing, and sang about God’s victory (Exodus 15:20–21).

She was not a background figure. The prophet Micah later named Miriam alongside Moses and Aaron as leaders God sent before Israel (Micah 6:4).

Then Numbers records a harder moment. Miriam and Aaron spoke against Moses because of his Cushite wife. They also asked whether God had spoken only through Moses and not through them (Numbers 12:1–2).

The text does not dismiss their leadership history. It does not let that history settle the dispute either. God confronted them, defended Moses’s particular calling, and Miriam was struck with a serious skin disease. Aaron admitted their wrongdoing, and Moses prayed for her healing (Numbers 12:4–13).

The whole community waited seven days until Miriam could return to the camp (Numbers 12:14–15). The leader who had once helped protect Moses now received protection, prayer, and time to recover.

I find both warning and mercy there. A strong contribution does not make my next judgment correct. If I have served for years, I may have more experience—but I also have more reason to listen carefully when trusted people challenge me.

Correction does not erase what came before. Miriam still helped save Moses. She still led the song. Scripture still remembers her as one of Israel’s leaders.

That gives me a better way to receive hard truth. I do not have to defend my entire life when one decision is questioned. I can examine the decision, own what needs owning, and accept help.

A faithful past is a gift. It is not immunity. Mature leadership keeps enough humility to be corrected and enough hope to return with grace.$pt$, 23, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2946, $q$The Burden You Did Not Choose$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2946$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/the-burden-you-did-not-choose$q$, $q$2027-04-11T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Some responsibilities arrive without asking whether we are ready. A family member becomes ill. A colleague leaves. A stranger needs help at the worst possible time. We did not choose the burden, but we still have to decide how we will carry it.

Simon of Cyrene came into Jesus’s story that way. Cyrene (sai-REE-neh) was a North African city near modern Shahhat, Libya, more than eight hundred miles west of Jerusalem. Mark identifies Simon as the father of Alexander and Rufus, but tells us nothing about why he was in Jerusalem that day (Mark 15:21). View the map for this story.

Jesus had been condemned to crucifixion. Roman soldiers led him out of the city toward Golgotha. Along the way, they forced Simon, who was passing by on his way in from the country, to carry Jesus’s cross (Mark 15:20–22).

Simon did not volunteer. The text does not tell us what he felt, what he believed, or whether he understood who Jesus was. It gives us one observable fact: imperial power pulled a bystander from the road and placed the wooden burden on him.

That matters because we sometimes make service sound like a moment of perfect willingness. Simon’s story is not that neat. He was interrupted, compelled, and placed inside someone else’s suffering.

Luke says the soldiers put the cross on Simon and made him carry it behind Jesus (Luke 23:26). The wording creates a picture Jesus had already used for discipleship: “Whoever wants to be my disciple must deny themselves and take up their cross daily and follow me” (Luke 9:23, NIV). Luke does not explain the connection, but the image is hard to miss.

Most of our unwanted burdens are not crosses, and another person’s emergency does not automatically become our permanent assignment. Limits still matter. Help can include calling someone better equipped, sharing the load, or saying what we cannot safely do.

But I do not want surprise to become my excuse for indifference. I can pause long enough to ask what care is possible now. Perhaps I can make the call, carry the bag, stay for the appointment, or take one task from someone who is exhausted.

Simon’s part in the story is brief, but it is not invisible. A burden he did not choose became an act remembered in three Gospels. Readiness did not come first. The next faithful step did.$pt$, 24, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2943, $q$Power Cannot Outsource Responsibility$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2943$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/power-cannot-outsource-responsibility$q$, $q$2027-04-04T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Avoiding a decision can feel safer than making the wrong one. But when I have the power to protect someone, silence is still a choice. The consequences do not disappear because I kept my hands clean.

Ahab (AY-hab) was king of Israel in the ninth century BC. He ruled from Samaria, in the hill country of today’s West Bank, and kept a royal residence at Jezreel, about twenty miles north. His wife, Queen Jezebel, strongly promoted the worship of Baal, while the prophet Elijah repeatedly called the nation back to God (1 Kings 16:29–33; 18:17–21). View the map for this story.

One story makes Ahab’s leadership painfully clear. A man named Naboth owned a vineyard beside Ahab’s palace at Jezreel. Ahab wanted it for a vegetable garden. Naboth refused because the land was his family inheritance (1 Kings 21:1–3).

Ahab went home angry, lay on his bed, turned his face to the wall, and refused to eat. When Jezebel asked what was wrong, he told her. She promised to get the vineyard for him (1 Kings 21:4–7).

She used Ahab’s royal authority to send letters in his name. The letters arranged false charges against Naboth. He was taken outside the city and killed. Then Jezebel told Ahab the vineyard was available, and he went down to claim it (1 Kings 21:8–16).

The text does not say Ahab wrote the letters or threw the stones. It also does not let him hide behind that fact. Elijah met him in the vineyard and announced judgment because Ahab had taken possession after Naboth’s murder (1 Kings 21:17–24).

That lands close to ordinary life. A supervisor may not invent the cruel joke, falsify the report, or exclude the difficult colleague. But if the supervisor knows, has authority, and chooses comfort over action, that quiet choice shapes the workplace.

I need to ask more than, “Did I cause this?” I also need to ask, “What could I have stopped?” Power includes responsibility for what I permit, reward, and quietly accept.

Ahab later humbled himself when he heard Elijah’s judgment, and God noticed that response (1 Kings 21:27–29). Even this story leaves room for a change in direction.

Leadership does not require controlling everything. It does require owning the choices that belong to me. If my name, position, or silence gives harm room to grow, I cannot outsource the responsibility for what follows.$pt$, 25, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1792, $q$The Watchman Who Kept Showing Up$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=1792$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/when-nobody-wants-to-hear-it-2$q$, $q$2027-03-28T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Waiting can make a person feel forgotten. Days keep moving while the answer, change, or relief we hoped for remains out of reach. The danger is not only disappointment; it is letting delay convince us that nothing faithful can happen now.

Ezekiel’s calling teaches that leadership is not validated only by immediate response. God asks his servants to bear witness faithfully.

Ezekiel was a priest among the Judean exiles in Babylon. By the Chebar canal, in the region of modern Iraq, he received visions and a prophetic commission after Jerusalem’s first deportation (Ezekiel 1–3).

God warned Ezekiel that his audience was rebellious and hard-hearted. The warning did not cancel the assignment: “Speak my words to them, whether they listen or fail to listen” (Ezekiel 2:7).

His ministry included symbolic actions, difficult messages of judgment, and visions that revealed both God’s holiness and Judah’s corruption. These signs were not theatrics for attention. They made visible the seriousness of covenant unfaithfulness and the certainty of coming judgment.

God called Ezekiel a watchman (Ezekiel 3:17; 33:7). A watchman cannot force people to respond to danger. His responsibility is to see clearly, sound the warning, and remain at his post. That distinction protects leaders from both despair and manipulation.

Ezekiel did not end with judgment. He proclaimed God’s promise of a new heart and a new spirit (Ezekiel 36:26), saw dry bones receive life by God’s breath (Ezekiel 37:1–14), and witnessed the glory of the Lord return (Ezekiel 43:1–5). Restoration belonged to God.

The leadership application is not that outcomes are unimportant. It is that obedience cannot be reduced to applause, attendance, or quick agreement. We must speak truth with humility, remain accountable to God, and trust him to bring life where human effort cannot.

A long search for work, an unresolved illness, or a strained relationship can narrow our attention to what has not happened. We still may be able to keep one promise, encourage one person, ask for help, or notice one good thing without pretending the pain is gone.

One useful pause is to ask what I am trying to protect and who may bear the cost. That question can expose a goal that needs to change before the decision is made.

Waiting is not proof that a life has stopped mattering. I want to stay honest about what hurts while remaining available to the good I can receive and give today.$pt$, 26, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1934, $q$The Message No One Wanted to Hear$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=1934$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/the-message-no-one-wanted-to-hear$q$, $q$2027-03-21T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Truth can become expensive when a lie is already protecting someone's comfort, reputation, or plan. The longer silence lasts, the easier it is to call avoidance wisdom. Yet hidden trouble usually grows while everyone pretends not to see it.

Spiritual decline rarely begins with a dramatic rejection of God. More often, it begins when worship becomes convenient and obedience becomes selective.

Malachi ministered in postexilic Judah, likely in the fifth century BC. The temple had been rebuilt, but renewed buildings had not produced renewed hearts.

Malachi confronted priests who offered animals that were blind, lame, or sick. His question exposed the issue: would a governor accept such a gift? (Malachi 1:8). The problem was not that they had stopped worshiping; it was that they had brought God what cost them least.

The people seemed genuinely surprised by the prophet’s charges. They asked how they had wearied God and how they had robbed him. That blindness is part of Malachi’s warning. Once compromise becomes normal, people can confuse religious activity with faithfulness.

Malachi called Judah to return with whole hearts: honesty in relationships, justice toward workers and the vulnerable, covenant faithfulness, and reverent worship. His teaching about the tithe (Malachi 3:8–10) was not a technique for prosperity. It pressed a deeper question: can God be trusted with what we hold most tightly?

He also pointed ahead. The Lord would come to his temple, preceded by a messenger who prepared the way (Malachi 3:1). The New Testament identifies John the Baptist in that prophetic stream.

Healthy leaders make room for the message that interrupts complacency. They examine not only whether the work is getting done, but whether love, integrity, and reverence still animate it.

Before asking what should change around us, Malachi invites us to ask what has grown careless within us.

This pressure can appear in a budget meeting, a medical conversation, or an apology that needs to be made. Telling the truth does not require cruelty. It asks us to be specific, own our part, and give the other person information they need to make a real choice.

One useful pause is to ask what I am trying to protect and who may bear the cost. That question can expose a goal that needs to change before the decision is made.

I want my words to do more than protect my image. Honest speech can be calm, careful, and kind. It can also be the first step toward repair that silence could never provide.$pt$, 27, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1958, $q$When Rescue Comes Through Someone Else$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=1958$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/when-rescue-comes-through-someone-else$q$, $q$2027-03-14T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Courage is easiest to admire after the danger has passed. In the moment, a faithful choice can look costly, lonely, and uncertain. We may know what should be done and still feel every reason to wait for someone else.

Othniel’s story begins the repeated cycle in Judges: rebellion, oppression, crying out, God’s rescue, and a season of peace.

Othniel, the son of Kenaz and related to Caleb, first appears as the man who captured Debir and married Caleb’s daughter Aksah (Joshua 15:16–17; Judges 1:12–13). He was already a proven warrior before he became Israel’s first judge.

Judges 3 tells us that Israel forgot the Lord and served the Baals and Asherahs. God gave them over to Cushan-rishathaim, king of Aram-naharaim, a Mesopotamian region associated with parts of modern Syria and Iraq. For eight years Israel lived under oppression (Judges 3:7–8).

When Israel cried out, the Lord raised up Othniel. The text is concise but decisive: “The Spirit of the Lord came on him, so that he became Israel’s judge and went to war” (Judges 3:10). Othniel defeated the oppressor, and the land had peace for forty years.

The point is not that Israel finally found a stronger personality. Their deliverance began with the Lord’s mercy and was carried out through a leader empowered by the Spirit. Othniel’s earlier courage mattered, but the text directs attention to God’s action.

This first cycle is also a warning. Israel’s suffering was connected to spiritual drift; rescue was not permission to return to the same patterns. Leaders serve people well when they help them name what has gone wrong, cry out honestly to God, and receive help without mistaking the helper for the Savior.

Othniel points beyond temporary relief to the need for a rescuer who can break the deeper cycle of sin. In Christ, God provides that ultimate deliverance. Until then, ordinary faithfulness and Spirit-empowered leadership remain gifts through which God brings help.

That moment may come when a coworker is treated unfairly, a vulnerable person needs help, or a decision is being hidden behind polite language. Courage does not require a dramatic speech. It may begin with one clear sentence, one practical offer, or one refusal to look away.

I may not control the result, but I can decide whether fear will make the choice for me. The next faithful step is often small enough to take and important enough not to postpone.$pt$, 28, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1954, $q$The Secret of Being Known$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=1954$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/the-secret-of-being-known$q$, $q$2027-03-07T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Courage is easiest to admire after the danger has passed. In the moment, a faithful choice can look costly, lonely, and uncertain. We may know what should be done and still feel every reason to wait for someone else.

The leaders who change the world aren't always the loudest voices in the room.

Sometimes they're the ones who stay close enough to learn what can't be taught.

John was a fisherman's kid who became part of history's most influential inner circle. He never called himself by name in his own writings—just "the disciple whom Jesus loved." Not bragging. Describing.

At the most important dinner in history, everyone else sat upright.

John leaned in.

Literally. His head rested on Jesus' chest (John 13:23). Not professional distance. Not strategic positioning. Physical closeness.

We talk about mentorship like it's a program. A curriculum. A series of meetings with agendas.

John understood something different.

Proximity beats curriculum.

When Jesus hung dying, he looked down and saw John standing there (John 19:26-27). Everyone else had scattered. John stayed close.

That's what closeness earns you. Not promotion. Responsibility.

Years later, exiled to a rocky island called Patmos, John had a vision that would become the book of Revelation (Revelation 1:9-20). Isolated from everything, he was still connected to what mattered.

Here's what we're learning together:

Identity isn't earned. It's received.

John never introduced himself as "the best disciple" or "the smartest follower." He identified himself by how he was loved.

When Peter wanted to know who would betray Jesus, he didn't ask directly. He asked John to ask (John 13:24). Because John was close enough to whisper.

On resurrection morning, John and Peter both ran to the empty tomb (John 20:1-10). John got there first. But he waited. He let Peter go in.

Confident people can wait.

John outlived all the other disciples. He had more time to reflect, more years to understand. And what did he write about most?

Love.

Not strategies. Not systems. Not five steps to anything.

Just love (John 21:20-24).

The leader who stays close learns what the distant observer never will.

Maybe the question isn't "How do I get ahead?"

Maybe it's "How do I stay close?"

That moment may come when a coworker is treated unfairly, a vulnerable person needs help, or a decision is being hidden behind polite language. Courage does not require a dramatic speech.

I may not control the result, but I can decide whether fear will make the choice for me. The next faithful step is often small enough to take and important enough not to postpone.$pt$, 29, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1946, $q$When Waiting Becomes the Work$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=1946$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/when-waiting-becomes-the-work$q$, $q$2027-02-28T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Waiting can make a person feel forgotten. Days keep moving while the answer, change, or relief we hoped for remains out of reach. The danger is not only disappointment; it is letting delay convince us that nothing faithful can happen now.

Waiting is not empty time when it is anchored in God’s promise. It forms the discernment needed for faithful action.

Luke describes Simeon as righteous and devout, waiting for Israel’s consolation. The Holy Spirit had revealed that he would see the Messiah before he died (Luke 2:25–26).

Simeon’s great moment began with an ordinary visit to the temple. Mary and Joseph came to fulfill the law after Jesus’ birth, bringing the offering available to families of modest means: two birds (Luke 2:22–24). There was no public announcement and no visible sign of power.

Yet Simeon recognized the child. He took Jesus in his arms and praised God: “My eyes have seen your salvation” (Luke 2:29–30). He saw not merely a private comfort but God’s light for the nations and glory for Israel.

The years of waiting had not made Simeon passive. They had made him attentive. He stayed close enough to God’s promise to notice its fulfillment in a form others could easily overlook.

Leaders often want a timetable, a clear result, and a visible role. Simeon offers another picture: keep showing up, keep praying, keep practicing obedience, and let God determine the moment and the scale of the assignment.

Faithful waiting does not guarantee that we will get everything we want. It does cultivate a heart able to receive what God gives. Simeon could release his life in peace because God had kept his word.

The question is not simply, “When will my opportunity come?” It is also, “What kind of person am I becoming while I wait?”

A long search for work, an unresolved illness, or a strained relationship can narrow our attention to what has not happened. We still may be able to keep one promise, encourage one person, ask for help, or notice one good thing without pretending the pain is gone.

One useful pause is to ask what I am trying to protect and who may bear the cost. That question can expose a goal that needs to change before the decision is made.

Waiting is not proof that a life has stopped mattering. I want to stay honest about what hurts while remaining available to the good I can receive and give today.$pt$, 30, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1930, $q$The Traitor Who Changed Everything$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=1930$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/the-traitor-who-changed-everything$q$, $q$2027-02-21T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Courage is easiest to admire after the danger has passed. In the moment, a faithful choice can look costly, lonely, and uncertain. We may know what should be done and still feel every reason to wait for someone else.

Rahab’s story does not celebrate deception or self-preservation. It shows a woman who recognized the Lord’s authority and staked her future on him.

Rahab lived in Jericho, a fortified Canaanite city near the Jordan River. Joshua 2 identifies her as a prostitute, placing her at the edge of respectable society and making God’s welcome especially striking.

When Israel’s spies entered Jericho, Rahab had already heard what the city had heard: the Lord had dried up the Red Sea and had defeated kings east of the Jordan (Joshua 2:9–10). Jericho’s leaders heard the reports and prepared to resist. Rahab drew a different conclusion: “The Lord your God is God in heaven above and on earth beneath” (Joshua 2:11).

Her faith did not remain private. She hid the spies and asked that her family be spared. The scarlet cord at her window marked the household that would be rescued when Jericho fell (Joshua 2:18–21; 6:22–25).

Rahab’s courage should not be flattened into a generic lesson about disruption. She entrusted herself to the God of Israel, separated from a doomed allegiance, and acted to protect those in her care. Hebrews remembers her for faith; James points to her actions as evidence that faith was real (Hebrews 11:31; James 2:25).

God brought Rahab into Israel, and Matthew includes her in Jesus’ genealogy (Matthew 1:5). Her past did not limit God’s mercy or purpose.

Leaders need Rahab’s clarity: listen carefully, name reality truthfully, and act on allegiance to God even when the surrounding system insists that nothing must change.

That moment may come when a coworker is treated unfairly, a vulnerable person needs help, or a decision is being hidden behind polite language. Courage does not require a dramatic speech. It may begin with one clear sentence, one practical offer, or one refusal to look away.

One useful pause is to ask what I am trying to protect and who may bear the cost. That question can expose a goal that needs to change before the decision is made.

I may not control the result, but I can decide whether fear will make the choice for me. The next faithful step is often small enough to take and important enough not to postpone.$pt$, 31, null, $q$waiting$q$)
