insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1911, $q$The Power of Stepping Back$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=1911$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/the-power-of-stepping-back$q$, $q$2027-02-14T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Courage is easiest to admire after the danger has passed. In the moment, a faithful choice can look costly, lonely, and uncertain. We may know what should be done and still feel every reason to wait for someone else.

The hardest leadership move isn't climbing higher. It's knowing when to make room for someone else. Real influence often grows by shrinking.

John the Baptist was a first-century leader who built one of the largest movements of his time. Crowds traveled miles to hear him speak. He had power, platform, and momentum. Then he gave it all away on purpose.

He had thousands of followers.

Political leaders feared him. Religious elites watched his every move. People came from everywhere just to be near him (Matthew 3:5-6).

Then one day, he pointed to someone else and said, "Follow him instead."

Wait. What?

Most leaders spend their whole lives building. Gathering. Growing. We measure success by how many people listen to us. How much influence we hold.

John did the opposite.

"He must increase, but I must decrease" (John 3:30).

This wasn't failure. This was strategy.

John understood something we easily miss: sometimes the best thing you can do is get smaller.

His team noticed. They came to him worried. "Everyone is going to him now" (John 3:26). They expected John to fight back. To compete. To protect his brand.

Instead, John smiled.

"A person can receive only what is given them from heaven" (John 3:27).

Here's what we're learning together:

The voice in the wilderness knew his job was to prepare the way (John 1:23). Not to be the way.

The best leaders know the difference.

They build things bigger than themselves. They prepare paths for others to walk. They find joy when someone else gets the spotlight.

John never wrote a book. Never built an empire. He was executed in a prison cell (Matthew 14:10).

But Jesus called him the greatest person ever born (Matthew 11:11).

Maybe greatness isn't about how big we become.

Maybe it's about making room for something greater.

That moment may come when a coworker is treated unfairly, a vulnerable person needs help, or a decision is being hidden behind polite language. Courage does not require a dramatic speech. It may begin with one clear sentence, one practical offer, or one refusal to look away.

I may not control the result, but I can decide whether fear will make the choice for me. The next faithful step is often small enough to take and important enough not to postpone.$pt$, 32, null, $q$waiting$q$)
