insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1928, $q$Who Stays When Everyone Leaves$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=1928$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/who-stays-when-everyone-leaves$q$, $q$2027-02-07T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Courage is easiest to admire after the danger has passed. In the moment, a faithful choice can look costly, lonely, and uncertain. We may know what should be done and still feel every reason to wait for someone else.

Mary Magdalene is remembered not for a title but for faithful presence at the moments when following Jesus was dangerous and costly.

Luke names Mary Magdalene among the women healed by Jesus and among those who supported his ministry from their resources (Luke 8:1–3). Scripture does not identify her with the unnamed sinful woman in Luke 7; her story deserves to stand on its own testimony.

At the crucifixion, many disciples had fled. The Gospels place Mary Magdalene among the women who remained near Jesus, witnessing his death and burial (John 19:25; Mark 15:40–47). She did not solve the crisis. She stayed close to the suffering of her Lord.

Then, before daylight on the first day of the week, Mary went to the tomb. John records her grief, confusion, and perseverance. When the risen Jesus called her by name, she recognized him. He sent her to tell the disciples that he was ascending to his Father and their Father (John 20:11–18).

Mary was not elevated because she outperformed the apostles. She was entrusted with the first resurrection announcement because she was present, attentive, and ready to obey. Her witness is part of the Gospel’s own account, not an embellishment.

In leadership, presence is often undervalued because it cannot be measured easily. Yet people remember who came near in grief, who remained when a project failed, and who kept showing up when there was no applause.

Mary Magdalene reminds us that courage is not always a speech or a strategy. Sometimes it is returning at dawn, grieving honestly, and listening for the voice of Jesus.

That moment may come when a coworker is treated unfairly, a vulnerable person needs help, or a decision is being hidden behind polite language. Courage does not require a dramatic speech. It may begin with one clear sentence, one practical offer, or one refusal to look away.

I may not control the result, but I can decide whether fear will make the choice for me. The next faithful step is often small enough to take and important enough not to postpone.$pt$, 33, null, $q$waiting$q$)
