insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1926, $q$The Power of Letting Go$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=1926$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/the-power-of-letting-go$q$, $q$2027-01-31T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Courage is easiest to admire after the danger has passed. In the moment, a faithful choice can look costly, lonely, and uncertain. We may know what should be done and still feel every reason to wait for someone else.

Mary’s “yes” to God was not passive resignation. It was courageous trust amid risk, uncertainty, and a future she could not manage.

Mary was a young Jewish woman from Nazareth, pledged to Joseph. Luke presents her as a recipient of grace, not as someone whose social status prepared her for public importance (Luke 1:26–38).

Gabriel’s announcement unsettled every expectation Mary had for her life. She would bear a son by the Holy Spirit, and that son would reign forever. Mary asked how this could be; she did not pretend to understand. Then she answered, “I am the Lord’s servant. May it be to me according to your word” (Luke 1:34–38).

That response did not remove the cost. It placed Mary’s reputation, relationships, and safety in God’s hands. Her song in Luke 1:46–55 shows that surrender is not weakness. She praised the God who lifts the humble, keeps his covenant, and overturns human pride.

Mary continued to live with mystery. She pondered the words and events surrounding Jesus’ birth (Luke 2:19, 51). At Cana she directed servants to do whatever Jesus said (John 2:5). At the cross she stood near her dying son (John 19:25–27). In each scene she neither controls Jesus nor withdraws from him.

Leaders often equate strength with having a plan and securing an outcome. Mary models a truer strength: discern what God is doing, obey what he asks, and remain present when the next step is painful or unclear.

Surrender is not indifference. It is active trust in the God whose purposes are wiser than ours.

That moment may come when a coworker is treated unfairly, a vulnerable person needs help, or a decision is being hidden behind polite language. Courage does not require a dramatic speech. It may begin with one clear sentence, one practical offer, or one refusal to look away.

One useful pause is to ask what I am trying to protect and who may bear the cost. That question can expose a goal that needs to change before the decision is made.

I may not control the result, but I can decide whether fear will make the choice for me. The next faithful step is often small enough to take and important enough not to postpone.$pt$, 34, null, $q$waiting$q$)
