insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1909, $q$When Your Best Move Becomes Your Worst$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=1909$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/when-your-best-move-becomes-your-worst$q$, $q$2027-01-24T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Harm often begins before anyone sees the damage. A private resentment, a protected lie, or a decision to use another person can grow until someone else pays the price. These stories matter because our choices never stay as private as we imagine.

Asa’s reign warns that success can quietly replace the humility that made faithfulness possible in the first place.

Asa ruled Judah for forty-one years. Early in his reign he removed idols, sought the Lord, and led reforms that brought peace (2 Chronicles 14–15).

When Judah faced a vast Cushite army, Asa prayed with clarity: “Help us, Lord our God, for we rely on you” (2 Chronicles 14:11). God gave Judah victory. Asa’s strength was not military superiority but dependence on the Lord.

Later, when Baasha of Israel threatened Judah, Asa chose a different path. He sent treasures from the temple and palace to Ben-hadad of Aram, asking him to break his treaty with Israel (2 Chronicles 16:1–6). The immediate problem eased, but the prophet Hanani confronted Asa for relying on a foreign king rather than on God.

Hanani’s rebuke reached deeper than one political decision. “The eyes of the Lord range throughout the earth to strengthen those whose hearts are fully committed to him” (2 Chronicles 16:9). Asa responded defensively, imprisoning the prophet and oppressing some of the people.

The contrast is sobering. The leader who sought God in a crisis later trusted resources, alliances, and control. Scripture does not make his illness a simple object lesson about medical care; it notes that in his disease he did not seek the Lord but only physicians (2 Chronicles 16:12).

Asa’s story invites leaders to ask: has competence replaced prayer? Has success made correction feel threatening? The practices that sustained a faithful beginning—humility, counsel, dependence, repentance—remain necessary to finish well.

A smaller version can happen at work or at home. We can hide a mistake, shift blame, exclude someone, or use sharp words to protect our standing. The immediate problem may move away from us, but it lands on a person who deserves honesty and care.

One useful pause is to ask what I am trying to protect and who may bear the cost. That question can expose a goal that needs to change before the decision is made.

Before I protect myself at someone else's expense, I want to stop and name the likely harm. Truth may cost me something now, but passing that cost to another person is not faithfulness.$pt$, 35, null, $q$waiting$q$)
