insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1950, $q$The Person Who Believed First$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=1950$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/the-person-who-believed-first$q$, $q$2027-01-17T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$A kind word can help someone keep going. Sometimes, though, they also need an introduction. They need someone to explain their work, answer a concern, or help them find a place to serve. Encouragement becomes useful when it helps a person take the next step.

Barnabas (BAHR-nuh-buhs) was a Jewish follower of Jesus from the island of Cyprus. His given name was Joseph. The apostles called him “son of encouragement” (Acts 4:36, NIV). He had already sold a field and given the money to help people in need.

Then he helped a man whom believers had good reason to fear.

Saul, later known as Paul, had threatened Jesus' followers and sought to arrest them. After encountering Jesus, he began preaching the faith he had attacked. But when he came to Jerusalem, the believers there did not trust that he had changed (Acts 9:1–28).

This was the same Jerusalem whose ancient center lies within and around today's Old City. Our map page locates that area. Acts does not identify the house where this meeting happened.

Barnabas brought Saul to the apostles, the church's leading witnesses to Jesus. He explained Saul's encounter with Jesus and his bold preaching afterward. Then Saul was able to stay with the believers and speak openly about his faith (Acts 9:27–28).

Barnabas gave them something to consider. He did not simply demand that they stop being afraid. He shared what had happened and brought the people together.

Later, when another church needed teaching, Barnabas went to find Saul and brought him into the work. They taught together for a year (Acts 11:25–26). His encouragement included both an introduction and a lasting partnership.

I notice how practical this is. It is easy to admire someone's ability from a distance. Helping them use it asks more of me. I may need to make a call, explain what I have seen, or spend time beside them.

That does not mean ignoring harm or promising that another person will never fail. Trust needs truth. An honest introduction can include limits as well as strengths.

Perhaps someone you know has done careful work that others have not seen. With their permission, share a specific example with someone who needs that help. Or invite them to contribute to a task you can do together.

Encouragement does not have to predict a great future. It can help someone take one real step today.$pt$, 36, null, $q$waiting$q$)
