insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1899, $q$The Kid They Almost Passed Over$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=1899$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/the-kid-they-almost-passed-over$q$, $q$2027-01-10T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$BIG IDEA

Sometimes the person least likely to lead is exactly who we need.

The nervous kid. The one who blushes. The stomach-ache before every meeting.

What if that's not a bug? What if it's a feature?

BACKGROUND

Timothy was a young man from a small town in ancient Turkey. His mentor, Paul, saw something in him that Timothy couldn't see himself. He went from a timid kid with a queasy stomach to leading one of the most influential movements in history.

STORY

He was crying again.

We know this because Paul mentions Timothy's tears in his final letter (2 Timothy 1:4). This wasn't a one-time thing. Timothy was emotional. Sensitive. The ancient world had a word for people like him.

Weak.

Yet Paul picked him anyway (Acts 16:1-3).

Here's what we often miss. Paul didn't choose Timothy despite his temperament. He chose him because of it.

Timothy had frequent stomach problems. Paul told him to drink a little wine for his ailments (1 Timothy 5:23). This wasn't casual advice. It was medicine for a man whose anxiety lived in his gut.

Sound familiar?

The thing is, nervous people notice things. They prepare more. They care more about getting it right.

Paul saw this.

"Don't let anyone look down on you because you are young," he wrote (1 Timothy 4:12). Notice what he didn't say. He didn't say, "Stop being nervous." He didn't say, "Toughen up."

He said, "Lead anyway."

Timothy's fear wasn't the enemy. His fear kept him humble. It kept him dependent. It kept him teachable.

The real enemy was letting others define what leadership looks like.

We've all been passed over. Too young. Too quiet. Too anxious. Too different.

But here's what Timothy teaches us: the qualities that make us feel disqualified might be the very things that make us effective.

Paul didn't need a tough guy. He needed a faithful one. Someone who would show up even when his hands were shaking. Someone who would speak even when his voice trembled.

"For God gave us a spirit not of fear," Paul reminded him, "but of power and love and self-control" (2 Timothy 1:7).

Timothy became one of the early church's most trusted leaders. Not because he conquered his anxiety. But because he refused to let it have the final word.

Maybe leadership isn't about feeling confident.

Maybe it's about showing up anyway.$pt$, 37, null, $q$waiting$q$)
