insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1890, $q$Your Side Hustle Is Your Superpower$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=1890$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/your-side-hustle-is-your-superpower$q$, $q$2027-01-03T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$BIG IDEA

The most influential leaders often aren't building platforms. They're building tents. Their day job becomes the doorway to something greater.

BACKGROUND

Priscilla and Aquila were first-century tentmakers. They ran a small business in Corinth. But their workshop became a training ground that shaped the early church.

STORY

A refugee couple opens a tent shop in a foreign city.

Then the most famous leader of their generation walks in. Needs a job.

They hire him.

That's how Paul met Priscilla and Aquila (Acts 18:1-3). Not in a temple. Not at a conference. In a leather shop, stitching fabric.

Here's what we tend to miss.

This couple didn't separate their work from their purpose. They fused them. Their business was the vehicle. Relationship was the fuel.

Later, a brilliant speaker named Apollos came to town. He was eloquent and passionate. But he had gaps in his knowledge.

Did they correct him publicly? Call him out? Post about it?

No.

They invited him to dinner (Acts 18:26).

Private correction. Public support. That's how you develop people without destroying them.

Paul later wrote that they "risked their own necks" for him (Romans 16:3-4). He called them "co-workers." Not followers. Partners.

And their home? It became a church (1 Corinthians 16:19).

Not a building with a budget. A living room with an open door.

We often think influence requires a stage. A title. A platform.

Priscilla and Aquila show us something different.

Influence grows through proximity. Through shared meals and honest conversations. Through showing up, day after day, with your door open.

They weren't waiting for permission to lead. They were already leading—with thread and needle, with food and hospitality, with quiet courage.

Maybe you don't need a bigger platform.

Maybe you need a longer table.$pt$, 38, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1884, $q$When Courage Looks Like Surrender$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=1884$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/when-courage-looks-like-surrender$q$, $q$2026-12-27T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$BIG IDEA

The strongest leaders aren't those who fight back the hardest.

They're the ones who know when to put down the sword.

Sometimes the most powerful thing we can do is refuse to defend ourselves.

BACKGROUND

Stephen was one of the first leaders chosen to serve the early church. He handled the daily distribution of food to widows (Acts 6:1-6). His job was logistics and care. Nothing flashy. But his life ended in a way nobody expected.

STORY

They said his face looked like an angel's.

Right there in the courtroom. With angry men shouting lies about him. With his life on the line. Stephen's face was calm. Radiant. At peace (Acts 6:15).

Think about that for a second.

When we're falsely accused, what happens to our face? Our body? Our voice?

We tense up. We prepare to fight back. We build our case.

Stephen did the opposite.

He stood before the most powerful men in his world. He told them the truth. And when they grabbed stones to kill him, he didn't run. He didn't beg. He didn't curse.

He looked up and said, "Lord, don't hold this against them" (Acts 7:60).

Sound familiar?

Jesus said the same words from the cross (Luke 23:34).

Stephen became the first person to die the way Jesus died. Not just physically. But spiritually. Forgiving his killers with his final breath.

Here's what we miss about this moment.

Stephen was smart enough to defend himself. He'd just delivered a brilliant speech about Israel's history (Acts 7:1-53). He knew the law. He knew the arguments.

He chose not to use them.

Because there's a moment when defending yourself stops being brave.

And surrender becomes the real courage.

In the crowd that day stood a young man named Saul. He watched everything (Acts 7:58). He approved of the killing.

Years later, that same Saul—now Paul—would write about a different kind of power. The kind that shows up in weakness (2 Corinthians 12:9).

Maybe he learned it from watching Stephen die.

We're told to stand our ground. Defend our reputation. Fight for what's ours.

But some victories can't be won by fighting.

Some can only be won by letting go.$pt$, 39, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2772, $q$Loyalty Needs an Honest Question$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2772$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/loyalty-needs-an-honest-question$q$, $q$2026-12-20T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$The people we support can hurt others with the help we give them. That is why loyalty needs room for an honest question. Before defending a friend or backing a leader, we can ask what our support is making possible.

Abimelech (uh-BIM-uh-lek) was a son of Gideon (GID-ee-uhn), an Israelite leader who had fought to free his people from an enemy army. After his father's death, Abimelech wanted power. He turned to his mother's relatives in Shechem (SHEE-kehm), a city in the hill country. In the NIV Bible, his name is spelled Abimelek.

Ancient Shechem stood near today's Nablus (NAH-blus), in the West Bank. The map page marks its ruins about a mile east-southeast of the modern city's center.

Abimelech asked the citizens to choose him rather than his many brothers. His family connection helped win them over. Their reason was simple: “He is related to us” (Judges 9:3, NIV).

They gave him silver. He used it to hire followers, then murdered his brothers. One younger brother escaped. After the killings, the city's people crowned Abimelech king (Judges 9:4–6).

The story does not leave responsibility with him alone. It also names the people of Shechem who helped him commit the murders (Judges 9:24). Their support had consequences beyond their own circle.

After three years, the alliance broke apart. Abimelech attacked the city that had backed him. More people died. His own violent rule ended during another attack, when a woman defending a tower struck him with a stone (Judges 9:22–54).

I find the early choice more unsettling than the final battle. Those citizens had a reason to feel close to him. But a family tie could not tell them whether his actions deserved their support.

Our disagreements are usually far less extreme. Still, that distinction matters. Knowing someone well does not settle whether a complaint about them is true. Sharing their goals does not excuse the way they treat another person.

We can care about someone and ask them to answer for a specific action. We can listen to the person who was hurt before preparing a defense. We can stop repeating a claim when we cannot support it.

The next time loyalty pulls you toward a quick defense, pause long enough to ask: What happened, and who is paying the cost? Caring about our people can include helping them face the truth.$pt$, 40, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1932, $q$Give Small Beginnings a Fair Chance$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=1932$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/give-small-beginnings-a-fair-chance$q$, $q$2026-12-13T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$People need room to begin before they can become skilled. A child's first page of writing will have mistakes. A new volunteer may need help with a simple task. If we judge only what is missing, we can miss the effort that deserves our care.

Zechariah (zeh-kuh-RYE-uh) spoke to people who were rebuilding. He was a prophet, someone called to deliver God's message. Around 520 BC, more than five centuries before Jesus, he encouraged a Jewish community that had returned after being forced from its homeland by Babylon, an empire based in today's Iraq.

They were rebuilding the temple in Jerusalem, their place of worship. Its site lies within the eastern side of today's Old City. The map and background page helps place that work in the city we know now.

Their governor, Zerubbabel (zeh-RUH-buh-behl), had laid the temple's foundation. Much remained unfinished. Through Zechariah, God promised that the same man would see the work through (Zechariah 4:9).

Then came a question: “Who dares despise the day of small things” (Zechariah 4:10, NIV).

Despise means to look down on something. In this case, the warning concerned work that had not yet reached its full size. The foundation mattered before the building was complete.

The passage also placed the work in God's hands. Success would depend on his Spirit, beyond the strength or power people could gather (Zechariah 4:6). This was a promise about their temple. It does not mean every project we start is certain to succeed.

Still, I hear a question worth bringing into ordinary life: Am I giving a beginning a fair chance, or measuring it only against a finished result?

That matters when someone brings us work they are still learning to do. We can name a sentence that communicates clearly, then help with the next one. We can show a volunteer how to do a task and stay nearby while they try it.

That kind of help is honest. It notices mistakes while making room for growth. Empty praise gives a learner little to use. A specific word of encouragement and a useful next step give them something to work with.

This week, look for one beginning you can support. Give it your attention before giving it a score. Help the person see what is already taking shape and what they can try next. Small work can deserve serious care.$pt$, 41, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2748, $q$Doubt Does Not Have to End the Conversation$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2748$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/doubt-does-not-have-to-end-the-conversation$q$, $q$2026-12-06T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$I want to be honest about what I find hard to believe. Hiding a question can leave me alone with it. Saying it aloud gives someone a chance to listen, and gives me a chance to learn something I could not see before.

Sarah had a question like that. She was Abraham's wife, and they had grown old without having a child together. God had promised Abraham a son through Sarah. By then, having a baby seemed beyond anything their bodies could do (Genesis 17:15–21).

One day, three visitors arrived at their camp near Mamre (MAM-ree), in the area of Hebron (HEE-bruhn). That city is in today's West Bank, about 19 miles south of Jerusalem. We do not know exactly where their tent stood.

Sarah listened from the tent entrance while the visitors ate. One said she would have a son about that time the next year. She laughed to herself. She and Abraham were old. The promise did not fit what she knew about her life (Genesis 18:1–12).

The Lord asked why she laughed. Afraid, Sarah denied it. The conversation exposed her doubt, but the promise remained. God had not made the future depend on her giving a polished answer (Genesis 18:13–15).

Later, Sarah gave birth to a son named Isaac (EYE-zuhk). She said, “God has brought me laughter” (Genesis 21:6, NIV). The same woman who had laughed in disbelief could now invite others to share her joy.

I do not read this as a promise that every longed-for outcome will happen. Sarah's story concerns a particular promise God made to her family. It gives me no reason to blame someone whose waiting ends differently.

What stays with me is the room between her first reaction and the rest of her story. Her first response was not her last word.

That matters when I feel certain a conversation will go badly, or assume that a person cannot change. I can name my concern without pretending I already know the ending. I can ask what I might be missing. I can listen for an answer instead of rehearsing my objection.

If a friend offers to make things right, I can ask what that would look like. A clear question leaves room for a real response.

Faith can begin with an honest sentence: I am having trouble believing this.

I want to leave room for the next sentence too.$pt$, 42, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1948, $q$Share the Hope You Notice$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=1948$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/share-the-hope-you-notice$q$, $q$2026-11-29T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Hope can be hard to carry alone. When someone notices a reason to keep going and shares it with me, the day can feel different. I want to offer that kind of encouragement: something honest and specific that helps another person see beyond the difficulty in front of them.

Anna was an elderly Jewish widow and prophet, a person who spoke for God. Luke introduces her at the temple, the great center of Jewish worship in Jerusalem. The temple stood on a raised site in the eastern part of what is now the Old City. The ancient temple building no longer stands.

Prayer and worship shaped Anna's life. Luke says she worshiped “night and day” (Luke 2:37, NIV). She had known the loss of her husband, yet the story shows her still taking part in the life of her people.

Then Mary and Joseph brought the infant Jesus to the temple, following their people's religious customs. Another worshiper had just praised God for the child when Anna came up to the family (Luke 2:22–38).

She thanked God. Then she spoke about Jesus to people who were hoping God would bring deliverance to Jerusalem. In other words, they were looking for God to rescue and restore his people (Luke 2:38).

Anna's hope became something she shared. Her part in the story was brief, but it reached beyond her own experience. She turned toward people who were waiting too.

I find that generous. She could give thanks and also help someone else see a reason for hope. The story makes me think about encouragement as a form of attention: noticing something worth naming, then taking the trouble to say it.

That can be simple. If someone is learning a difficult task, I can point to a skill they have gained. If a friend is worn down by caring for others, I can name the care I see and offer help. Specific words give encouragement something solid to rest on.

I do not need to promise that everything will turn out well. I can listen to what is hard and still recognize what is good. Both can belong in the same conversation.

Anna reminds me that a reason for hope is worth sharing. Today, I want to notice one real thing that could encourage someone and tell them what I see.

It may be a small gift. It is still a gift I can give.$pt$, 43, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2725, $q$Let People Be More Than a Label$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2725$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/let-people-be-more-than-a-label$q$, $q$2026-11-22T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$I want to leave room for people to change. If I reduce someone to a job, a mistake, or a reputation, I may stop listening before they speak. A label can become the only thing I see, even when a person is taking a new step.

Matthew was a tax collector who became one of Jesus's twelve closest followers. When Jesus called him, he was sitting at his collection booth. This was near Capernaum (kuh-PERR-nay-uhm), a lakeside town in what is now northern Israel. Its ruins stand on the northwestern shore of the Sea of Galilee.

Tax collectors worked within the system of rulers who controlled the region. Their work brought them suspicion and resentment. The story does not list Matthew's personal offenses. It does show how strongly some people objected to sharing a table with people in his line of work.

Jesus spoke two words to Matthew: “Follow me” (Matthew 9:9, NIV). Matthew stood up and went with him.

The next scene is a meal. Jesus and his followers ate with tax collectors and others described as sinners. Some religious teachers questioned why Jesus would eat with such people (Matthew 9:10–11).

Jesus answered by comparing his work to a doctor's care. People who are sick need help. He also pointed the teachers toward mercy (Matthew 9:12–13). He made room at the table for people who needed a different direction in life.

What catches my attention is that Jesus addressed Matthew as someone who could respond. Matthew's occupation was part of his story, but it did not prevent an invitation. The response belonged to Matthew, and he took it.

That challenges the way I might describe someone else. Calling a coworker unreliable can end the conversation before I ask what happened. I can name the missed deadline clearly, explain its effect, and ask what would help us keep the next agreement. The problem stays visible. So does the person.

Leaving room for change does not require instant trust. Trust can grow as someone follows through. I can pay attention to that evidence instead of repeating an old judgment every time their name comes up.

Matthew's story invites me to look again. Is there someone I have stopped seeing because I think I already know the whole story?

I want my next conversation to leave room for an answer I have not heard yet.$pt$, 44, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2718, $q$Give Care a Practical Form$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2718$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/give-care-a-practical-form$q$, $q$2026-11-15T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$When someone depends on me, love needs a practical form. Concern matters, but it cannot make a meal or arrange a ride by itself. I want to notice what care requires and do the part that is within my reach.

Jochebed (JAHK-uh-bed) was the mother of Moses, who would later lead the Israelites out of slavery in Egypt. His life began under a threat. Egypt's king had ordered that newborn Hebrew boys be thrown into the Nile River (Exodus 1:22).

Her story belongs to the world of the Nile Delta in northern Egypt, where the river spreads toward the sea north of modern Cairo. The Bible does not identify the exact riverbank where she acted.

Jochebed hid her baby for three months. When she could no longer keep him hidden, she made a basket from river plants and sealed it against the water. She placed him among the reeds at the river's edge. His older sister watched from a distance (Exodus 2:1–4).

The king's daughter found the crying child and felt compassion for him. His sister offered to find a woman to nurse him. She brought back his own mother. Jochebed cared for her son until he was older, then brought him to the king's daughter, who adopted him (Exodus 2:5–10).

Hebrews remembers that Moses's parents acted “by faith” (Hebrews 11:23, NIV). Their faith had something to do with the way they protected a child.

I notice how practical the care was. There was hiding, a basket made ready, and a place chosen among the reeds. Exodus describes a child placed at the bank, with his sister watching. Each detail matters because this was a real life in danger.

The story makes me look at the gap between feeling concerned and offering useful help. A person caring for a sick relative may need groceries brought to the door. A neighbor with a broken car may need a ride. I can ask what would help most and listen carefully before deciding what to offer.

I cannot promise to solve everything. I can be clear about what I can offer, agree on a time, and follow through. That makes my help something another person can count on.

Jochebed's story leaves me with a simple question: what does care need to become in my hands today?

I want to answer with something I can actually do.$pt$, 45, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2704, $q$Leave Room for an Answer$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2704$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/leave-room-for-an-answer$q$, $q$2026-11-08T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$When I ask someone for help, I want to understand something I may have missed. That takes more than asking a question. I need to leave room for an answer that could change what I do next.

Cornelius (kor-NEE-lee-us) was a Roman army officer. He lived in Caesarea (see-zuh-REE-uh), a port on the coast of present-day Israel, about thirty miles north of Tel Aviv. He prayed regularly and gave generously to people in need (Acts 10:1–2).

An angel told him to send for Peter, one of Jesus's followers. Cornelius sent messengers and gathered his relatives and close friends. He was ready to hear what Peter would say (Acts 10:3–8, 24).

This meeting crossed a real divide. Peter was Jewish. Cornelius was not. Before the visit, God had shown Peter that he should not treat another person as unclean. Peter came into the house, and Cornelius explained why he had sent for him (Acts 10:28–33).

The officer did not use the gathering to give orders. He told Peter that they were there before God to listen. Peter, too, had something to learn. He said, “God does not show favoritism” (Acts 10:34, NIV).

Peter spoke about Jesus's life, death, and resurrection. As he spoke, the Holy Spirit came upon those listening. Peter called for them to be baptized. They asked him to stay for a few days (Acts 10:36–48).

What stays with me is the room Cornelius made for that message. His work involved authority. In his home, he took the place of a learner. His prayers led to a practical response: he sent for someone, welcomed him, and listened.

I can ask for advice and still have my defense ready before the other person finishes. I can invite someone to explain a problem while hoping they will confirm the answer I already prefer. The question sounds open. My response may close it.

Cornelius gives me a different picture. Being responsible for others does not mean I have nothing left to receive. I can listen without losing my responsibility to think carefully about what I hear.

At the next conversation where I need help, I can ask a clear question and let the answer finish. Then I can name what I learned and decide what needs to change.

I want my willingness to learn to show up in what I do afterward.$pt$, 46, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2698, $q$Make Room for Someone Else's Joy$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2698$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/make-room-for-someone-elses-joy$q$, $q$2026-11-01T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$I want the people I love to feel free to bring me their good news. That means I need to welcome it without turning the conversation back to myself. Their joy can deserve my full attention even when I have something important happening, too.

Elizabeth (ee-LIZ-uh-beth) gives me a picture of that welcome. She was the mother of John the Baptist, who would later prepare people for Jesus. Before John was born, she and her husband had reached old age without children. Luke describes them as faithful to God (Luke 1:5–7).

Then Elizabeth became pregnant. She spoke of the pregnancy as God's kindness to her after the shame she had experienced among other people (Luke 1:24–25).

During her sixth month, her relative Mary came to visit. Mary was carrying Jesus. Elizabeth lived in the hill country of Judea (joo-DEE-uh), the region around Jerusalem. Christian tradition remembers their meeting at a site about five miles from Jerusalem's Old City, though Luke does not name the town.

When Elizabeth heard Mary's greeting, her baby leaped. Filled with the Holy Spirit, she called Mary blessed and welcomed her as the mother of her Lord. She said, “Blessed is she who has believed” (Luke 1:45, NIV). Mary stayed with her for about three months (Luke 1:39–56).

Elizabeth's own life was changing. Yet her words made room for what God was doing in Mary's life. She named it aloud. Her welcome did not require Mary to make her news smaller. Both women could give thanks in the same home.

That welcome asks something simple of me. When a friend shares good news, I can stay with their story. I can ask what it means to them. I can resist following their first sentence with a longer account of my own experience.

I do not have to pretend that every part of my life is settled before I celebrate with someone. Joy and an unanswered hope can exist in the same person. I can be honest about my own life and still offer a kind, interested response.

For me, that might begin with putting down my phone and asking one more question. It might mean naming the work I saw someone put in, or simply saying that I am glad they told me.

I want my attention to say what Elizabeth's welcome showed: there is room here for your joy.$pt$, 47, null, $q$waiting$q$)
