insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2687, $q$When Faith Becomes a Bargain$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2687$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/when-faith-becomes-a-bargain$q$, $q$2026-10-25T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$Faith matters to me because I need help seeing beyond what I want. But I can turn prayer into a bargain: I will do the right religious things, and God will make my plans work. That leaves little room for God to change the plans—or me.

Micah (MY-kuh) was a man who lived in Israel before it had kings. He is not the later prophet whose name appears on a Bible book. His home was in the hills of Ephraim (EE-fray-im), part of the central hill country north of Jerusalem, in today's West Bank. Its exact location is unknown.

His story starts with stolen silver. Micah admitted taking it from his mother and returned it. She then used some of it to have an idol made. It went into his house, where he had his own place of worship (Judges 17:1–5).

Later, Micah hired a young man from Israel's priestly tribe to serve as his priest. Micah took this as a reason to expect God's favor: “Now I know that the Lord will be good to me” (Judges 17:13, NIV).

The next chapter tests that confidence. Armed men from the tribe of Dan took his religious objects. They offered his priest a job serving their whole tribe, and the priest went with them. Micah protested, but the men threatened him. Seeing that they were stronger, he went home (Judges 18:14–26).

The story does not make the theft right. It shows how little protection Micah's arrangement gave him. The things he had gathered, and the priest he paid, could be carried away.

I see a warning here about treating faith as something I can arrange around my own wishes. A gift to a church does not buy God's agreement. A prayer before a decision does not mean I have listened for an answer I might dislike.

For me, listening begins with an honest question: am I willing to change course? I can read the passage that challenges me, hear the concern I would rather dismiss, and admit when a choice serves me at someone else's expense.

I still want to bring my hopes to God. I also want to leave room for correction. Faith can help me face an uncertain outcome without pretending I have purchased a certain one.

That is a different prayer from asking God to protect every plan I have already made.$pt$, 48, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2676, $q$Listen Before You Add to the Load$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2676$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/listen-before-you-add-to-the-load$q$, $q$2026-10-18T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$When someone tells me a task has become too much, I want to hear more than a complaint. They may be telling me something I cannot see from where I sit. If I have the power to change their workload, listening is part of my responsibility.

Rehoboam (ree-ho-BO-am) faced that choice when he succeeded his father, King Solomon, about 3,000 years ago. People gathered to make him king at Shechem (SHEE-kehm). The ancient city's site is near modern Nablus (NAH-blus) in the West Bank.

The people asked for relief from the harsh labor Solomon had required. They offered to serve the new king if he lightened their burden. Rehoboam asked them to return in three days while he sought advice (1 Kings 12:1–5).

His father's older advisers urged him to serve the people and answer their request favorably. But the men who had grown up with him urged a harsher reply. He chose their advice. When the people returned, he threatened to make their work even harder.

The account puts it plainly: “the king did not listen to the people” (1 Kings 12:15, NIV). The northern tribes rejected his rule. Rehoboam fled to Jerusalem, and the kingdom split (1 Kings 12:16–20).

I do not read this as a rule that older people are always right or younger people are always wrong. I notice what each group asked the king to do. One urged him to respond to the people's burden. The other urged him to increase it.

That gives me a useful question when I seek advice: does this help me understand the people affected, or only help me defend my decision?

At work, I could ask which task takes the most time before adding another deadline. At home, I could ask what needs to come off the list before asking someone to do more. Then I need to let the answer change something.

Listening does not mean I can grant every request. It does mean I can explain a real limit without treating the person who asked as a problem. I can name what I can change and follow through on it. A smaller promise kept can matter more than a quick reassuring answer.

Rehoboam had three days to consider his answer. I may have time to pause, too. Before I ask someone to carry more, I want to understand what they are already carrying.$pt$, 49, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2666, $q$An Apology Needs a Next Step$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2666$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/an-apology-needs-a-next-step$q$, $q$2026-10-11T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$When I hurt someone, saying I am sorry is a beginning. The person I hurt also has to live with what I do next. If I keep making the same choice, my words ask them to carry the cost of a change I have not made.

Manasseh (man-ASS-eh) was a king of Judah, the small kingdom centered on Jerusalem. He ruled in the seventh century BC, more than 2,600 years ago. The city remains in the same hills today.

His story includes terrible harm. He brought the worship of other gods into God's temple. He sacrificed his children and caused the deaths of innocent people. These were acts with victims, not just errors in judgment (2 Chronicles 33:3–6; 2 Kings 21:16).

The book of Chronicles says God warned Manasseh and his people, but they did not listen. An invading army captured him and took him to Babylon. That ancient city's ruins are in modern Iraq, about 53 miles south of Baghdad.

In captivity, Manasseh asked God for help. The account says he “humbled himself greatly” (2 Chronicles 33:12, NIV). God heard his prayer and brought him back to Jerusalem.

Then his actions began to change. He removed the idols and the altars he had built for other gods. He restored the Lord's altar and called the people to worship God (2 Chronicles 33:15–16).

That change matters. So do the lives he had already harmed. His return could not make those deaths unhappen. The book of Kings keeps his violence in the record. I do not want to rush past those people to reach a comfortable ending.

What I see in Manasseh is a reason to stop defending a wrong choice. I can admit what I did without first finding someone whose actions were worse. I can ask God for mercy and still accept responsibility for repair.

For me, that might mean correcting a false statement in front of the people who heard it. It might mean returning money, changing a decision, or asking what would help without demanding an immediate answer. The next step should address the actual harm.

The person I hurt may need time or distance. My apology does not give me the right to set their timetable for trust.

I cannot rewrite what happened. I can stop adding to it. That is where I want an apology to become visible: in a different choice, made again when no one is applauding.$pt$, 50, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2656, $q$Remember Who Helped You$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2656$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/remember-who-helped-you$q$, $q$2026-10-04T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$I want to notice the people whose daily work makes my life possible. A meal, a finished project, a safe place to sleep: someone helped make each of those happen. When I forget that help, I can start treating everything I have as something I produced alone.

Nabal (NAY-bul) was a wealthy livestock owner in ancient Judah. His sheep were being sheared at Carmel (KAR-mul), a town usually identified with a site about eight miles south-southeast of Hebron (HEE-brun), in today's West Bank.

David, who would later become Israel's king, was living in the wilderness with his followers. They had helped protect Nabal's shepherds. At shearing time, David sent men to ask Nabal for food. He invited Nabal to check with his own workers about the help they had received (1 Samuel 25:4–8).

Nabal refused and insulted David. In his reply, he spoke of “my bread and water” and the meat prepared for his workers (1 Samuel 25:11, NIV). His answer made no room for the people who had helped keep his flocks safe.

David responded by preparing an armed attack. Nabal's insult did not excuse that choice. People throughout the household were now in danger.

One servant went to Nabal's wife, Abigail (AB-ih-gayl). He described the protection David's men had given them. She gathered food, met David, and urged him to stop. David accepted her appeal and turned back (1 Samuel 25:14–35).

While she was preventing bloodshed, Nabal was holding a feast. He did not learn what had happened until morning. The account ends with his death about ten days later (1 Samuel 25:36–38).

I do not read this as a promise that every refusal will bring disaster. What stays with me is how much Nabal's workers knew that his answer ignored. He had people nearby who could have helped him see the whole picture.

Before I decide that someone wants too much, I can ask what they have already contributed. Before I take credit, I can name the person who did the work. A thank-you becomes more useful when it is specific, and when fair treatment follows it.

I can still have limits. I cannot meet every request. But I want my answer to begin with an honest picture of what I have received. Remembering who helped me is one way to keep gratitude from becoming just a word.$pt$, 51, $q$Nabal$q$, $q$done_before$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2642, $q$Let Someone Tell You the Truth$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/?p=2642$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/let-someone-tell-you-the-truth$q$, $q$2026-09-27T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, $pt$I want the people I lead to be safe telling me when I am wrong. If keeping my respect means hiding a problem, my position has become part of the problem. I need someone who cares enough to speak, and I need to make room to listen.

Nathan (NAY-thun) was a prophet, someone who delivered God's messages. He advised David, the king of ancient Israel. Their story takes place in Jerusalem, the city that still stands in the hills about 32 miles east of the Mediterranean coast.

David had slept with Bathsheba (bath-SHEE-buh), another man's wife. When she became pregnant, David tried to hide what he had done. He then arranged for her husband, Uriah (you-RYE-uh), to die in battle. Afterward, David took her as his wife (2 Samuel 11).

God sent Nathan to confront him. Nathan began with a story about a rich man who owned many animals. A poor neighbor had one lamb, raised with his children and loved like part of the family. When a visitor arrived, the rich man took the poor man's lamb for the meal instead of using his own (2 Samuel 12:1–4).

David was furious. He could see the cruelty in someone else's choice.

Then Nathan said, “You are the man!” (2 Samuel 12:7, NIV).

Nathan named what David had done. The king's power did not remove his responsibility. David admitted his sin. Nathan told him that God had forgiven him, but serious consequences would follow (2 Samuel 12:9–14).

That is what I find hard about this story. I can spot unfairness quickly when someone else is responsible. When a complaint points toward me, I can become more interested in explaining myself than understanding the hurt.

My choices are not David's choices. But I can still listen for the moment when my explanation becomes an excuse. Did I interrupt someone? Did I take credit for work I did not do? Did I make it costly to disagree with me?

I can start by asking a person I trust about one specific decision. Then I can let them finish. I can check what happened, acknowledge my part, and change what I do next. An apology matters more when the person I hurt can see the difference.

Nathan gave David a chance to face the truth. I want to welcome that chance before someone has to fight to be heard.$pt$, 52, $q$Nathan$q$, $q$done_before$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (2627, $q$Smart Don't Mean Good$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/smart-dont-mean-good/$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/smart-dont-mean-good$q$, $q$2026-09-20T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$publish$q$, $pt$I want my advice to help people, not just help someone win. That means asking why a plan matters before working out how to make it succeed. A smart plan can still hurt someone.

Ahithophel (uh-HITH-uh-fel) helped David, the king of ancient Israel, make hard choices. People trusted his advice. But when David's son tried to take the throne, Ahithophel chose the son's side (2 Samuel 15:12).

The son's name was Absalom (AB-suh-luhm). He wanted to replace his father as king. David fled Jerusalem, and Absalom entered the city with his followers. This was the same Jerusalem we can find today, in the hills about 32 miles east of the Mediterranean coast.

Ahithophel had a plan. He would take twelve thousand men and chase David that night. David was tired and weak. Ahithophel would kill him and bring his followers back to Absalom. The leaders who heard the plan liked it (2 Samuel 17:1–4).

The man who once helped David now offered to hunt him down.

But another adviser spoke. Hushai (HOO-shy) was David's friend. David had sent him back to work against Ahithophel's advice. Hushai urged Absalom to wait, gather more soldiers, and lead the army himself. Absalom chose his plan instead (2 Samuel 15:34; 17:5–14).

The Bible says God had decided to stop “the good advice of Ahithophel” (2 Samuel 17:14, NIV).

That phrase can be confusing. Here, I understand “good” to mean the plan could work. It does not mean killing David was right. Ahithophel knew how to win. He was using that skill to help a son kill his father.

I can see a smaller version of that problem in daily life. A sharp reply might win an argument and wound a friend. Leaving someone out might make a project easier and cost that person a fair chance. Getting the result I want does not settle whether I should want it.

So before I offer advice, I need to name the purpose. Who would this help? Who might it hurt? Would I still support it if I were the person paying the price?

Those questions give me a way to pause. I can change the goal, suggest a kinder path, or refuse to help with harm.

I want to bring more than a clever answer to the table. I want a reason for giving it that I can stand behind.$pt$, 53, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1907, $q$The Prayer That Worked$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/the-prayer-that-worked/$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/the-prayer-that-worked$q$, $q$2026-09-13T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$publish$q$, $pt$Hezekiah is around forty years old, fourteen years into his reign, when Isaiah walks in with a message that has no conditions attached to it. Put your house in order. You are going to die. You will not recover (2 Kings 20:1).

Hezekiah turns his face to the wall and prays. It is a short prayer, and it is about his record: remember how I have walked before you faithfully and with wholehearted devotion (2 Kings 20:3). Then he weeps bitterly.

Isaiah does not make it out of the middle court before God turns him around. I have heard your prayer. I have seen your tears. Fifteen years (2 Kings 20:4-6).

That is usually where this story gets told from, and it should be. A stated outcome undone by one man weeping at a wall. I have no interest in shrinking that.

But keep reading, because the failures the text lingers on all happen inside those fifteen years.

The Babylonians came because he got well. The delegation arrived with congratulations on his recovery, and he walked them through the whole treasury, and Isaiah told him what it would cost: all of it carried off to Babylon, and his own descendants with it, to serve in a foreign palace (2 Kings 20:17-18).

Hezekiah's answer is the part I had to read twice. "The word of the LORD you have spoken is good." Because he thought: there will be peace and security in my lifetime (2 Kings 20:19).

He heard that his grandchildren would be hauled off in chains and filed it under good news, because the dates worked out.

Manasseh was born in there too. He was twelve when Hezekiah died (2 Kings 21:1), which on a plain reading of the numbers puts his birth about three years into the extension. He went on to reign fifty-five years and take apart most of what his father built.

The Chronicler, who is never shy, names it exactly. Not "he sinned later." This: "Hezekiah's heart was proud and he did not respond to the kindness shown him" (2 Chronicles 32:25).

He does get the last word, and it's a mercy. Hezekiah repented of the pride of his heart, and the judgment was held back for the rest of his life (2 Chronicles 32:26).

Real repentance. It just came after the tour.

I was thirty-five when a doctor told me I had months.

My prayers got very small after that, and very specific. Please let me live. That was the whole prayer. I was not asking for wisdom about the time or clarity about what I'd do with it. I was asking to have it.

It was granted.

And it arrived the way it arrived for Hezekiah. No handbook. Nobody sat me down afterward to explain what the extra years were for, or what I now owed, or how a person is supposed to carry something like that. I got the years and I got the same silence he got.

Which is why the Chronicler's line is the one that gets me. He did not respond to the kindness shown him. That is not a failure I would have thought to watch for in myself. It doesn't feel like a failure. It feels like going back to work.

I don't have to imagine what Hezekiah prayed with his face to that wall. I know what I prayed with my face to mine. Same prayer.

The asking turned out to be the easy part.

I've had a lot of years since. The question I'm still working on is not whether I wanted them.$pt$, 54, $q$Hezekiah$q$, $q$done_before$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1875, $q$When Outcomes Stop Mattering$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/when-outcomes-stop-mattering/$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/when-outcomes-stop-mattering$q$, $q$2026-09-06T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$publish$q$, $pt$They (Shadrach, Meshach, & Abednego) were teenagers when Babylon took them — maybe fifteen, marched out of Jerusalem with the rest of the useful boys. Twenty years later they were middle-aged men running a province for the king who took them. Foreign administrators in a foreign government, loyal to their captors and faithful to their God. They had careers by then. They had something to lose.

Then Nebuchadnezzar built a statue ninety feet tall and called the whole administration out to the plain of Dura.

When the music played, everyone bowed. The penalty for standing was fire.

Three men stayed on their feet.

Here's where it gets interesting. The king offered them a second chance — bow now, or burn — and mocked their God on the way out: "What god will be able to rescue you from my hand?" (Daniel 3:15).

Their answer is the whole story.

"We do not need to defend ourselves before you. If we are thrown into the fire, the God we serve is able to deliver us. But even if he does not, we will not serve your gods" (Daniel 3:16–18).

Even if he does not.

I make most of my decisions on expected outcomes. I run the odds. I hedge. I commit with an exit already picked out.

These three cut the outcome out of it. Their obedience wasn't priced against God's intervention. They weren't betting on a rescue. They were saying who they were.

The furnace got heated seven times hotter. The soldiers who carried them died at the mouth of it. Then there was a fourth figure walking in the flames (Daniel 3:25), and the three came out with their clothes intact and no smell of smoke on them. Hebrews 11:34 remembers them as the ones who "quenched the fury of the flames."

They couldn't have known any of that was coming. The miracle came after the commitment, not before.

That fourth figure is the part I keep sitting with. Not someone who kept them out of the fire. Someone who was in it with them.

The decisions that have defined me are the ones where I couldn't see the ending. Making the right call when it's guaranteed to work has never cost me anything, and it has never told me anything about myself either.

"Even if he does not" is the posture of someone who already did the math. Counted the cost. Accepted the worst case. Decided their integrity outweighed their survival.

That's not recklessness. That's clarity.

When you take the outcome out of the equation, you find out what you actually believe.$pt$, 55, $q$Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego$q$, $q$done_before$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1952, $q$When Doubt Steals Your Voice$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/when-doubt-steals-your-voice/$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/when-doubt-steals-your-voice$q$, $q$2026-08-30T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$publish$q$, $pt$He had prayed for a son every day of his adult life.

Zechariah was an old priest, married to Elizabeth, and they were well past the age where the prayer made any practical sense. His division served at the temple two weeks out of the year. The incense offering was assigned by lot, and with hundreds of priests in the rotation, most got one turn in a lifetime. Plenty got none at all.

That morning the lot fell to him (Luke 1:9).

The single most sacred moment of his career. And while he's standing there in the Holy Place, an angel appears at the right side of the altar. Gabriel himself. "Your prayer has been heard. Your wife Elizabeth will bear you a son."

Zechariah's response?

"How can I be sure of this?" (Luke 1:18)

Wait.

He prayed for this. For decades. And now he's asking for proof.

Gabriel doesn't argue. He takes the man's voice. Nine months of silence.

That struck me as harsh, until I sat with it a while.

Zechariah's doubt wasn't curiosity. It was the thing I do — ask for something, then act surprised when it shows up. I've prayed for a door to open and then stood in the doorway questioning whether I was ready to walk through it. I've asked for the opportunity and spent the first week checking to see whether it was real.

The silence wasn't punishment. I've come to think it was an invitation.

Nine months to stop talking and start trusting. Nine months to watch the impossible unfold in his own house — Elizabeth further along every week, and no way to explain it to a soul. Nine months to get ready for what he'd been asking for all along.

When the boy was born, the neighbors assumed he'd carry his father's name. Elizabeth said no. John. They turned to the old man and handed him a tablet.

He wrote: "His name is John" (Luke 1:63).

His voice came back that instant.

But he wasn't the same man. The first words out of his mouth weren't complaints, and they weren't questions. They were worship — a prophecy spoken over the baby about preparing the way (Luke 1:67-79).

The silence had done its work.

Here's what I'm learning. My biggest obstacle usually isn't getting the thing I asked for. It's believing it when it arrives.

Sometimes the forced pause is what gets you ready to receive it.$pt$, 56, null, $q$waiting$q$)
