insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1903, $q$When Nobody Is Left to Tell You No$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/when-nobody-is-left-to-tell-you-no/$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/when-nobody-is-left-to-tell-you-no$q$, $q$2026-08-16T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$publish$q$, $pt$Most of us do not need more people who agree with us. We need at least one person who can tell us the truth when agreement would be easier.

That becomes harder as our influence grows. A leader may have advisers, employees, friends, and family nearby and still have no one who feels free to say, “You are wrong.”

King David learned the value of that voice after committing adultery with Bathsheba and arranging the death of her husband, Uriah. The prophet Nathan came to him with a story about a rich man who stole a poor man’s only lamb. David was furious at the injustice. Then Nathan made the story personal: “You are the man!” (2 Samuel 12:7).

Nathan’s words carried risk. David had already used his power to hide what he had done. Yet Nathan spoke, David confessed, and the truth interrupted the cover-up.

David’s son Solomon later became famous for wisdom. He organized a large government, built the temple in Jerusalem, and drew visitors from other nations. First Kings even records the names and jobs of his chief officials (1 Kings 4:1–6).

But success did not protect Solomon from drift. Early in his reign, he married the daughter of Egypt’s pharaoh as part of a political alliance (1 Kings 3:1). Later he accumulated many foreign wives, worshiped other gods, and ignored repeated warnings (1 Kings 11:1–10). After his death, the kingdom split.

The Bible does not say that Solomon lacked honest advisers. Still, the contrast makes me stop. David’s worst failure was met by a person who stood in the room and named it. In Solomon’s long decline, Scripture records warnings, but no similar scene of a trusted person breaking through.

That raises uncomfortable questions for us. Who can challenge a decision before it becomes a pattern? When did we last change course because someone spoke honestly? What happened to the last person who disagreed with us?

We may say we welcome correction. The people around us learn the truth by watching our response. If disagreement brings punishment, distance, or ridicule, silence will soon look safer.

Wisdom is not only knowing what to do. Sometimes it is giving another person enough standing to tell us what we cannot see—and enough safety to keep telling us.

That kind of honesty is worth protecting.$pt$, 58, $q$Solomon$q$, $q$done_before$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1886, $q$Leave the Stage for One$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/leave-the-stage-for-one/$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/leave-the-stage-for-one$q$, $q$2026-08-02T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$publish$q$, $pt$BIG IDEA

What if the day you're needed most is the day God tells you to leave?

I chase scale. I measure impact by crowd size.

But the moment that outlived Philip came when he walked away from the crowd—for one person on an empty road.

BACKGROUND

Philip was a first-century leader, chosen to help distribute food to the widows of the early church (Acts 6:5). Later he's known simply as "the evangelist" (Acts 21:8).

His story exposed something I'd missed about influence.

STORY

Picture it. A city is buzzing. Crowds gather daily. Lives are changing.

Philip is in Samaria (modern-day West Bank, near Nablus). The momentum is electric (Acts 8:5–8).

Everything a leader dreams of is happening.

Then an angel of the Lord speaks. Go south—to the desert road that runs from Jerusalem down toward Gaza (modern-day Gaza Strip, on the coast) (Acts 8:26).

Wait—what?

Leave the revival?

Walk away from the crowds?

Trade the stage for a dusty road and a stranger?

He goes.

No fanfare. No crowd. Just Philip—and then the Spirit tells him to run to a passing chariot (Acts 8:29–30).

Inside sits an Ethiopian official (from the kingdom of Kush, in modern-day Sudan). Powerful. Searching. Puzzling over a scroll of Isaiah he can't unlock.

Philip climbs in.

They talk.

The official believes, and is baptized right there on the road (Acts 8:35–38).

THE TWIST

Here's what I missed.

That one conversation carried the gospel toward Africa. Tradition holds the Ethiopian church traces its roots to this encounter. Years later, Philip's own four daughters would prophesy in his home (Acts 21:9). The obedience he lived on that desert road became the air they grew up breathing.

But Philip couldn't have known any of that.

He didn't leave Samaria thinking, I'm about to reach a continent. He didn't climb into that chariot to change generations. He climbed in because God said go—and he went. The ripple was never his to see.

That's the part that stops me.

I used to think obedience was about the outcome—the crowd, the continent, the story someone tells later. It isn't. In the moment, the outcome was never mine to hold.

Have I lived my own small version of this? I don't know. I may never know whether the conversations and relationships I'm in will change anything for the generations after me. But when I look in the rearview mirror, I can see the hand of God. Miracles, worked through ordinary moments. In the moment, though? They felt like normal conversations. Nothing electric. Nothing that announced itself.

Maybe that's the point for me. Be obedient in the moment. Leave the rest up to God.

My friend Clare De Graaf wrote a whole book on this—The 10-Second Rule. The idea is simple: when you sense God nudging you toward something, do the next thing you're reasonably sure He's asking—within ten seconds, before you talk yourself out of it. Not because you can see where it leads. Because He asked.

Philip had his ten seconds on a desert road.

The crowd in Samaria? Others came to serve them—Peter and John followed close behind (Acts 8:14).

The man on the desert road? Only Philip was there.

This one person changed history—though Philip never lived to see how far it went.

Maybe today's interruption isn't a distraction from the call.

Maybe—when it's God who redirects me—it's the whole point.

I don't need to see the ripple. I need to be obedient in the moment… it makes me think.$pt$, 59, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1905, $q$When the Manual Finally Shows Up$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/when-the-manual-finally-shows-up/$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/when-the-manual-finally-shows-up$q$, $q$2026-07-26T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$publish$q$, $pt$Do you remember your third-grade teacher's name?

Hold onto that picture for a second. A kid that age. Times tables. Recess. A backpack that's a little too big.

Now imagine this.

You're in third grade, and your dad gets killed — by the people who work for him. His own staff.

Then it gets stranger. The people of your town hunt down the ones who did it, and kill them too.

Now the part that rearranges your whole life: your dad was the king. Which means the job doesn't sit empty.

It's yours.

You. Third grade. Running a country.

One more thing you should know, because it matters. Your family has history. Your grandfather ran the place for fifty-five years, and he was, by every account, a bad dude — the kind who spends half a century tearing down the very thing the country was built on. And your father, the one who just got killed? Two years on the job, and already headed down the same road.

That's the family. That's the inheritance.

That's where Josiah starts.

Now roll the clock forward. He's twenty-six.

And here's what he's doing at twenty-six: he's running a renovation. The national temple — the most important building in the country — is getting restored. Rotten timber pulled out. Foundation repaired. Crews on payroll. Budget managed. Real, visible progress.

Then a worker finds something buried in the walls.

A scroll.

Not a memo. Not a copy of last year's plan. This was the founding document — the original charter that spelled out who these people were, what they were for, and how they had agreed to live. Every law. Every promise. The whole reason the nation existed in the first place.

It was the answer to "why are we even here," written down once, at the beginning — and then lost.

Missing for decades. Buried inside the very building built to hold it. Gone so long that when they finally read it out loud, there's a good chance not one person in that room had ever heard the words.

An entire nation had been running — collecting taxes, fighting wars, renovating its own temple — without the one document that told it who it was.

Josiah tears his clothes.

Not a figure of speech. He literally rips them.

Because it lands on him all at once: they hadn't just misplaced a book. They'd lost the plot. The building looked incredible, and nobody inside it could have told you what it was for anymore. The renovation was skin-deep. The drift went all the way to the bottom.

Here's what's easy to miss.

Josiah wasn't lazy. He wasn't asleep at the wheel. He was already doing good work. The renovation was real. People were employed. The budget balanced. The system ran.

But good work and right work aren't always the same thing.

I can run an efficient operation pointed in the wrong direction. I can polish a process that shouldn't exist. I can hit every number and miss the entire point.

This was my first corporate job, in the late 1980s — back when artists didn't have computers. That's almost impossible to picture now, but it's true. Computers were for accountants. A creative department ran on drawing boards, pencils, wax machines, and X-Acto knives.

So moving about three dozen board artists onto machines wasn't a software update. It was asking a room of people to give up their hands.

I took it seriously. I hired a consultant from my alma mater to run the transition — pulled her in from Art Center, where she was leading the exact same digital shift. We poured ourselves into their morale, their pace, the tablets that they would need, proper chairs to sit on because the board were being removed, whether the whole change would break them.

And with all that care, all that expertise in the room, I missed the most obvious thing. Production went to a standstill. They couldn't type.

I totally missed it.

I'd handed a team of career artists a tool they couldn't physically operate. So before we made a single beautiful thing on those Macs, they all went back to typing school.

That was my scroll. Not a vision. Not a strategy. Typing — the one thing so obvious I'd stepped right over it.

And that's the thing about a scroll. It rarely goes missing because it's rare. It goes missing because it's basic — because it sits underneath everything, which is exactly why you stop seeing it.

Josiah didn't have to go acquire anything new. The scroll was in the building the whole time. He just had to rediscover what was already his — and then find the guts to hold everything he'd built up against it.

So here's the only question that matters.

Not: are you busy?

Have you found the scroll?

Or better said, have I found the scroll?

Note: The whole story lives in two parallel accounts: 2 Kings 22–23 and 2 Chronicles 34–35. Chronicles tells it with a bit more detail on the reforms; Kings is the tighter narrative.$pt$, 60, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1873, $q$When Your Boss Wants Your Soul$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/when-your-boss-wants-your-soul/$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/when-your-boss-wants-your-soul$q$, $q$2026-07-26T16:00:00Z$q$, $q$publish$q$, $pt$BIG IDEA

Some jobs want more than your talent. They want your identity. The question is not whether you will face this pressure. It is whether you will recognize it when it comes.

BACKGROUND

Daniel was a teenager when enemy soldiers dragged him from Jerusalem to Babylon. He spent the next seventy years working for kings who worshipped false gods. He rose to the top. He never bowed.

STORY

In high school, I lived in Iran. One trip took us to Shush — ancient Susa — to the Tomb of Daniel, the site where he has been venerated for over a thousand years. I stood at the tomb of a man who spent his whole career in a system built to own him, and walked out with his soul intact.

Here is how that system worked.

They changed his name.

Day one in Babylon, and the regime erased his identity. Daniel, meaning "God is my judge," became Belteshazzar, meaning "Bel protect the king." A foreign god stamped onto his forehead (Daniel 1:7).

This is how I've watched organizations go after souls. Not with chains. With small surrenders.

And the surrenders come in different packages. Some are obvious. Most are not.

The king's food looked harmless. Premium meals. Executive dining. But Daniel saw it for what it was: a loyalty test. Eating meant belonging. He resolved not to defile himself (Daniel 1:8).

So he drew a line.

Not a loud line. Not a protest. Vegetables and water, please. A refusal that could have cost him everything — the chief official feared the king would have his head over it (Daniel 1:10). Daniel drew it anyway.

I have drawn that line. Years ago I oversaw national TV campaigns, and more than once the agency creatives pitched concepts that leaned on sexual innuendo — sometimes tongue-in-cheek, sometimes direct. When I rejected them outright, the agency didn't shrug and move on. They went over my head. They pleaded to my superiors that I was a prude, working to sabotage my own authority. One time it worked. I was overruled, and the compromise was to take the concepts — one of them flatly R-rated — out to focus groups. The public echoed my original direction. Some went further: they said if that campaign aired, they would never buy from the brand again. Ever.

Drawing the line looked like it cost me nothing. It nearly cost me my seat at the table.

And here is what fascinates me about Daniel. He drew his line and excelled anyway. The king found him ten times better than all the magicians in the realm (Daniel 1:20). I assumed for years that compromise was the price of success. Daniel proved otherwise.

Decades later, rivals tried to destroy him — and the only weapon they could find was his faithfulness. Three times daily, Daniel prayed toward Jerusalem. Windows open. No hiding (Daniel 6:10). The lion's den was supposed to end him. Instead, the king came out proclaiming Daniel's God as the living God (Daniel 6:26).

Daniel's faithfulness did not remove him from Babylon. It positioned him at the center of it. Four kings. Dreams interpreted. Policy shaped. An inner life Babylon could not touch.

The lesson I take is not about food or lions.

It is about knowing which battles to fight. Daniel picked his. He lost the name fight. He won the soul fight. You may not get to keep your title. Keep your heart.$pt$, 61, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1871, $q$Build While They Circle$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/build-while-they-circle/$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/build-while-they-circle$q$, $q$2026-07-19T16:00:00Z$q$, $q$publish$q$, $pt$BIG IDEA

The best leaders don't wait for perfect conditions.
They build with one hand and hold a sword with the other.
Progress happens when you refuse to come down for your critics.

BACKGROUND

Nehemiah served as cupbearer to the king of Persia. The title sounds like a servant's job. It was a seat of trust beside the most powerful man alive. He gave it up to rebuild a city's broken walls. His story is about leading under pressure while enemies circled (Nehemiah 1–6).

STORY

Here's what nobody told me about building something that matters.

I expected silence. I got sabotage.

Nehemiah learned this the hard way. He left everything to rebuild Jerusalem's walls. Within days, his enemies started mocking. Then they plotted to attack the city (Nehemiah 4:8). Eventually they schemed to harm Nehemiah himself (Nehemiah 6:2).

Most leaders would've called a meeting. Maybe formed a committee. Perhaps asked for better timing.

Not Nehemiah.

He armed his workers. Half built while half stood guard. Every builder wore a sword (Nehemiah 4:17–18).

This is the posture I'm learning to embrace.

Building and defending at the same time. Progress and protection. Creating while critics circle.

I've seen it in my own life. I have long-time friends who say nothing when things are status quo. But when I've stepped into ministries and nonprofits pushing the world toward a better place, they suddenly find their voices. A few have pulled back to the point of alienation. When I take a stand, they get noisy.

Here's what surprised me most: the sword I need isn't for them. It's for my own thoughts. Ridicule from people that close conjures doubts, and the doubts do more damage than the critics ever could. I've caught myself asking, "Were they ever really my friends?" — when the real question is simpler. Is my job to stay focused?

The opposition didn't stop for Nehemiah either. They invited him to "talk things over" four times. Each invitation was a trap (Nehemiah 6:2–4). And the noise came from close — Tobiah had family ties inside Jerusalem itself (Nehemiah 6:17–18). Nehemiah's response is legendary:

"I am doing a great work and I cannot come down."

There it is.

The work itself becomes the answer to every distraction. I'm learning that the mission protects me from meaningless debates — including the ones I hold with myself.

Fifty-two days. That's how fast the wall went up — because Nehemiah wouldn't come down (Nehemiah 6:15).

After the wall was finished, something beautiful happened. The people gathered. They read Scripture aloud for hours. They wept. They celebrated (Nehemiah 8:9–12).

The wall was never really about the wall.

I read those chapters and see something else: space for restoration. For worship. For community to return to its roots.

We're all building something. The critics came for Nehemiah. They've come for most builders I've watched. The invitations to "come down" multiply.

Keep your sword. Keep your trowel. Keep building.$pt$, 62, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, post_url = excluded.post_url, substack_url = excluded.substack_url, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, post_text = excluded.post_text, priority = excluded.priority;
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, post_url, substack_url, publish_date, post_status, post_text, priority, character, status) values (1869, $q$The Silent Power of Perfect Timing$q$, $q$https://stevesammons.com/the-silent-power-of-perfect-timing/$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/the-silent-power-of-perfect-timing$q$, $q$2026-07-12T16:00:00Z$q$, $q$publish$q$, $pt$I don't get to pick my defining hour. Neither did Esther. The question I keep circling isn't whether that moment will come—it's whether I'll be ready when it does.

Background

Esther was a Jewish orphan who became queen of Persia. An unlikely leader with no credentials except courage and timing. Her story shows me that preparation happens in the dark, long before the spotlight found her.

The Story

She had one shot.

One conversation with a king who hadn't asked to see her. One audience that could end in her execution.

The law was clear. Anyone who approached the king uninvited faced death—unless he extended his golden scepter (Esther 4:11).

Esther hadn't been summoned in thirty days.

But her people faced genocide. A decree had been signed. Every Jew in the empire would be destroyed (Esther 3:13).

Her cousin Mordecai sent her a message that changed everything:

"Who knows whether you have come to the kingdom for such a time as this?" (Esther 4:14)

Here's what I missed the first dozen times I read this.

Esther didn't storm in demanding change. She didn't lead with her agenda. She threw a dinner party.

Actually, two dinner parties (Esther 5:4-8).

She waited. Built relationship. Created space for the right moment to emerge.

The leaders I've most wanted to imitate weren't the boldest ones in the room. They were the positioned ones—patient, watching, ready.

Between those two dinners, the king couldn't sleep. He read the royal records. He discovered Mordecai had once saved his life (Esther 6:1-3).

Timing Esther couldn't manufacture. Circumstances no one in the story could orchestrate.

At the second banquet, Esther finally spoke. She revealed the plot. She named the villain. The king acted immediately (Esther 7:3-10).

Her people were saved.

What strikes me about Esther is how her story points beyond itself. A deliverer rises from obscurity. An innocent stands in the gap for the guilty. Someone risks everything so others might live.

Centuries later, another would do the same—not just risking death, but embracing it.

Here's where I have to be honest, though. I may never get an Esther-sized moment. No throne room. No empire hanging on one conversation.

But I've stopped believing that lets me off the hook.

Long before the banquets, Esther was being watched. She won favor with everyone who saw her, back when nothing was at stake (Esther 2:15). The big moment found her because the small ones had already shaped her.

My kids are watching how I react when the day goes sideways. The people in my organization are watching how I behave when I think no one important is in the room.

So I've set a personal goal, and I keep it in front of me every day: I want the distance between how I behave when things are going well and how I behave when things are topsy-turvy to be very close.

How am I when things aren't going my way?

I learned something about that in college, driving a rental car through the desert between LA and Vegas. The tire blew out. I opened the trunk and found the spare flat and shredded. I was renting from a cheap place, and I didn't have much extra money — but I'd expected a spare. What followed was one adversity after another, and a live demonstration, running in my head the whole time, of whether I could keep myself in check.

A phrase surfaced out there that still comes back to me: "Well, didn't want that to happen."

But so what. It happened. Now what — that's the important part.

The world watched Esther.

The world is watching me.

Your "such a time as this" might not be a moment at all. It might be this afternoon, with an audience of one.$pt$, 63, null, $q$waiting$q$)
